eek\\":\\"2026-W34\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-08-17T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-08-24T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-08-17\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-08-23\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgTqafcQXtEwZdrpihTGC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at this athlete's current mileage of 37.7 with a 13-mile long run, training for a November marathon with a 3:30 goal, I'm thinking carefully about progression given their shin history. I'm sketching a week with easy days, one quality session, and a long run bumped to 14 miles, keeping total volume increase modest around 40 miles, while noting I should ask about heat conditions for the long run.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WjXqU2dZPGMZyZeUfAjJSL","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"analysis":"## Why this week looks the way it does\\n\\nYou handled 37.7 miles with an honest long run and the aerobic signal is clean, so the next step is a small build to about 40 with one controlled tempo. Easy HR has stayed at 144 while easy pace nudged from 9:50 to 9:43, which is exactly the direction you want for a marathon base.\\n\\nThursday is a tempo rather than reps because you flagged the 800s felt too fast early. Running sustained 4 miles near 8:15 – 8:25 per mile teaches pacing discipline and gives a cleaner read on where fitness sits versus 3:30 goal pace of 8:00/mi. The Endorphin Speed 4 is the right shoe for it.\\n\\nThe long run grows to 14 miles at the same easy effort you ran Sunday. If the air is thick at 6am again, drop pace by 10 – 20 seconds per mile from the start rather than hanging on and drifting late, which is what the +6.9 bpm drift on Sunday hinted at.\\n\\nMileage is up roughly 2 miles on last week, well inside a safe ramp given your shin history. Keep the two strength sessions in and the sleep closer to 7.5 if you can; it is the cheapest gain available right now.","headline":"You are building cleanly toward Lakeshore: nudge to about 40 miles this week with a controlled tempo on Thursday and a 14-mile long run kept at the same easy effort you ran Sunday.","flags":[{"title":"Tempo pacing discipline","detail":"Last Thursday's 800s ran 6:36 – 6:41/mi, faster than needed. This week's tempo is deliberately steadier at 8:15 – 8:25/mi."},{"title":"Long-run HR drift in heat","detail":"Sunday drifted +6.9 bpm with back half even on pace. If humid, start 10 – 20 sec/mi slower rather than fading late."},{"title":"Shin history","detail":"Mileage ramp held to ~2 mi this week. Flag any shin tightness early rather than training through it."},{"title":"Sleep","detail":"6.9 hr average is on the low side given the build. Aim for 7.5 where travel allows."}],"answers":[{"question":"Should I slow the long run down when it is this hot, or hold the pace?","answer":"Slow it down from the first mile, do not try to hold pace. In real heat, 10 – 20 seconds per mile slower keeps the aerobic stimulus the same while cutting the drift and the late-run slog you felt Sunday. Target the same easy heart rate (around 148, staying under 150), and let pace fall where it falls. Fitness is built by time at effort, not by splits you fought for in the humidity."}],"prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W34","days":[{"date":"2026-08-17","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest day. Travel day, nothing to prove."},{"date":"2026-08-18","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy shakeout to open the week. Keep HR under 150 and let pace be whatever that is.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 135 – 148","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","structure":"5 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-08-19","dayType":"easy","notes":"Standard midweek aerobic run before Thursday's tempo. Nothing creeping into Z3.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":12875,"paceOrHr":"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 13