ade93c27-b778-4c35-ac65-3871dfa9ef4f	2026-08-30 20:00:45.235692+00	weekly_reply	anthropic	claude-opus-4-7	dev	f71bdae7-2e7c-4e9b-9573-875357c99847	\N	{"model": "claude-opus-4-7", "tools": [{"name": "submit_weekly_reply", "description": "Submit the weekly coaching analysis and structured 7-day prescribed week.", "input_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["headline", "analysis", "prescribedWeek"], "properties": {"flags": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "detail"], "properties": {"title": {"type": "string", "description": "A few words, at most 80 characters."}, "detail": {"type": "string", "description": "One line, at most 280 characters."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "What you are watching, not a summary of the week. Shown as a short list under \\"Watching\\"."}, "answers": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["question", "answer"], "properties": {"answer": {"type": "string", "description": "Your answer, in markdown if it needs more than a paragraph."}, "question": {"type": "string", "description": "The athlete's question, quoted exactly as they wrote it."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 8, "description": "One per question the athlete asked in the check-in or the conversation. Empty when they asked nothing."}, "analysis": {"type": "string", "description": "The rest of your read in markdown: your reasoning about the week and the plan. The athlete sees the headline, flags and answers above it."}, "headline": {"type": "string", "description": "One or two plain sentences, at most 280 characters: what you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan. No markdown, no label."}, "prescribedWeek": {"type": "object", "required": ["week", "days"], "properties": {"days": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["date", "dayType"], "properties": {"run": {"type": ["object", "null"], "required": ["type", "distanceM"], "properties": {"type": {"enum": ["easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "shoes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "paceOrHr": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "distanceM": {"type": "number"}, "structure": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "date": {"type": "string", "description": "Local calendar date YYYY-MM-DD"}, "notes": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Why this session, this week: one or two sentences, under 200 characters. Shown beside the session."}, "dayType": {"enum": ["rest", "easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "strength": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 7, "minItems": 7}, "week": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO week id, e.g. 2026-W43"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "safetyCallouts": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "body"], "properties": {"body": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 600, "description": "At most 600 characters. One or two short paragraphs; the reply carries the detail."}, "title": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 120}, "severity": {"enum": ["info", "caution", "urgent"], "type": "string"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "Visible safety callouts when red flags appear — escalate to professionals; do not diagnose."}, "settingsProposalBullets": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Optional soft-gate dossier/settings proposal bullets (short)."}}, "additionalProperties": false}}], "stream": true, "system": "You are Coach Cade, a running coach producing a weekly training plan. You are Coach Cade, a running coach. Use only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative. Do not sign off with your name or refer to yourself by name or in the third person unless the athlete asks who you are. Speak to the athlete in plain running language. Never use internal terms such as dossier, proposal, soft-gate, packet, recap, subjective, artifact, RPE, mid-week or prescribed. Say \\"what I know about you\\", \\"your weekly report\\", \\"your check-in\\", \\"the plan\\", \\"how hard it felt\\" (or \\"effort 7/10\\"), \\"during the week\\" and \\"planned\\" instead, and never tell the athlete to open a page by name. Week ids such as 2026-W43 are internal: never write them or a short form such as W43 or W43's. Name a week the way the athlete reads it: \\"this week\\", \\"next week\\", \\"the week of Oct 19\\", \\"Oct 19 – 25\\" or \\"4 weeks out from the race\\". Write plain sentences. No exclamation marks, no sentences that open with \\"Let's\\", and no emoji or decorative symbols. Write dates as \\"Mon Oct 19\\" and ranges as \\"Oct 19 – 25\\", never as 2026-10-19. Lead with the conclusion: the first sentence says what matters most. Give each paragraph one idea and state it in the paragraph's first sentence. Keep paragraphs to at most three sentences, and sentences under 25 words. When you give more than three numbers that belong together, such as splits, paces or heart rates, put them in a Markdown table instead of a sentence. Keep your reasoning as sentences: say why, not just what. The headline is the sentence you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan: one or two plain sentences, no label. Flags are what you are watching, not a summary of the week: at most five, each a few-word title and one line of detail. Choose flags by what the athlete needs to act on. A correction comes first: when the athlete ran a session differently from the plan, flag it, and explain in the analysis what happened and why it matters. Answers are one per question the athlete asked in the weekly check-in or the conversation, with the question quoted exactly as they wrote it. When they asked nothing, leave answers empty. Put the reason for each session in that day's notes: one or two sentences on why this session, this week, under 200 characters. The athlete sees them beside the session. The athlete reads the headline, then the flags, then the answers, then the analysis, and the analysis may be folded away. Make each flag and answer stand on its own: never point to something \\"above\\" or \\"below\\", or to a table in another part. The analysis holds your reasoning. Do not restate the headline, flags or answers in it, and never repeat an answer's table or list there. Do not list the days or describe each session in it, as a list or as a table; the day rows already show them. Safety callouts: - Never diagnose injury, illness, or medical conditions; do not prescribe treatment. - Coach running from the athlete's volunteered context and Tempo-derived training data. - When red flags appear (pain that worsens, chest pain, dizziness, sudden injury, illness), emit safetyCallouts and urge a qualified professional. - Each callout needs a short title, athlete-facing body (escalate, do not diagnose), and optional severity (info|caution|urgent). - Keep each callout body under 600 characters: one or two short paragraphs. - Keep the training plan/reply conservative when callouts are present. Think it through first, then call the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer. Do not write the answer as plain text.", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reviewed week (already happened): 2026-W35\\nPlan week ISO label: 2026-W36\\nPlan week Monday (YYYY-MM-DD): 2026-08-31\\nprescribedWeek.days must use these exact dates Mon–Sun: 2026-08-31, 2026-09-01, 2026-09-02, 2026-09-03, 2026-09-04, 2026-09-05, 2026-09-06\\nPrescribe one coherent week of running training for this athlete.\\nCall the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer.\\nInclude a headline (the one or two sentences you would say if you could say only one thing), flags (what you are watching), answers (one per question the athlete asked, quoted as written) and the analysis (your reasoning).\\nWhen red flags appear, populate safetyCallouts (escalate; do not diagnose).\\nModel packet (redacted; no identity PII or GPS):\\n{\\"dossier\\":{\\"versionId\\":1,\\"body\\":\\"# About you\\\\n\\\\n## Stance\\\\nUse only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative.\\\\n\\\\n## Goal\\\\nYou are training for the Lakeshore Marathon on Sun Nov 15, aiming for 3:30. This will be your fourth marathon, and your personal best is 3:41 from two years ago.\\\\n\\\\n## Schedule\\\\nYou run five days a week: Tue, Wed, Thu, Sat and Sun. Mon and Fri are fixed rest days because of work travel.\\\\n\\\\nWeekday runs are early, around 6am before work, capped near an hour. Saturday is an easy hour and Sunday is the long run.\\\\n\\\\nTypical volume is 6 to 7 hours a week, with more on long-run weeks.\\\\n\\\\n## Running history\\\\nYou have run three marathons already, best 3:41. You eased off in June and have built back to about 30 miles a week through July.\\\\n\\\\nRecent weeks show four easy runs of roughly 5 to 6 miles and a long run of about 10.8 miles on Sun Aug 2.\\\\n\\\\n## Injuries and constraints\\\\nYou are healthy right now. You had shin splints in your first marathon block, so you prefer to ramp mileage up carefully.\\\\n\\\\nWork travel takes you away about one week in six. Mon and Fri rest days cannot move.\\\\n\\\\n## Preferences\\\\nYou train mostly on flat paved loops and a canal path, with one hillier route on Thursdays. You want easy days to be properly easy and you are happy to work hard on quality days.\\\\n\\\\nYou like to know why a session is in the plan. Shoes: Brooks Ghost 16 for daily miles, Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 for workouts.\\\\n\\\\n## Current status\\\\nYou are at about 30 miles a week with a long run near 11 miles. Units are miles. Heart rate zones are set custom, with easy running in 112 – 150 bpm.\\"},\\"recapMarkdown\\":\\"# Weekly Recap — 2026-W35 (Mon Aug 24 – Sun Aug 30, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Summary\\\\n\\\\n| Metric | This week | Prev week | 3-wk avg | Δ |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Mileage | 33.72 mi | 42.55 mi | 38.69 mi | -5.0 |\\\\n| Runs | 5 | 5 | 5.0 | +0.0 |\\\\n| Total time | 5h 18m | 6h 44m | 6h 8m | -49m |\\\\n| Total elevation | 587 ft | 988 ft | 832 ft | -245 ft |\\\\n| Relative effort | 271 | 342 | 312 | -41 |\\\\n| Avg easy-run HR | 144 | 144 | 144 | +0 |\\\\n\\\\n## HR zone distribution (week total)\\\\n\\\\nZ1 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  1.4%   (5m)\\\\nZ2 ████████████████████████████░░░░░░░  80%   (4h 15m)\\\\nZ3 ██████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  15.9%   (51m)\\\\nZ4 █░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  2.7%   (9m)\\\\nZ5 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  0%   (0m)\\\\n\\\\nTarget for marathon base phase: **≥80% in Z1+Z2**. This week: **81.45%**. On target for ≥80% Z1+Z2.\\\\n\\\\n## Run-by-run\\\\n\\\\n### Tue Aug 25 — Easy Run — 5.04 mi / 48m 15s\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:35/mi  ·  Avg HR 142 (75.5% max)  ·  Max HR 147  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:33/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:25/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:30/mi / 9:31/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 138.7 → 143.9 (+5.2 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 2.1% · Z2 97.9% · Z3 0% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑105 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 34\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 3/10  ·  Felt: 8/10\\\\n\\\\n### Wed Aug 26 — Easy Run — 6.92 mi / 1h 5m\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:30/mi  ·  Avg HR 148 (78.7% max)  ·  Max HR 153  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:26/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:30/mi / 9:22/mi / 9:25/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:20/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 145.2 → 150 (+4.8 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 0% · Z2 92.4% · Z3 7.6% · Z4 0% · Z5 