 · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑154 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 34\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 3/10  ·  Felt: 7/10\\\\n\\\\n### Sun Aug 9 — Long Run — 12.08 mi / 1h 57m\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:43/mi  ·  Avg HR 148 (78.7% max)  ·  Max HR 156  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:31/mi / 9:31/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:49/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:39/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 143.9 → 151.3 (+7.4 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 0.4% · Z2 80.1% · Z3 19.5% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑187 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 82\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 6/10  ·  Felt: 6/10\\\\n\\\\n### Long run — Sun Aug 9 — 12.08 mi / 1h 57m\\\\n\\\\nAvg pace: 9:43/mi  ·  Avg HR 148  ·  Drift: 143.9 → 151.3 (+7.4 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:31/mi / 9:31/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:49/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:41/mi / 9:44/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:39/mi\\\\n\\\\nBack half avg pace: 9:40/mi vs front 9:38/mi (vs front half; Δ -2s/mi)\\\\n\\\\nLast 3 mi at marathon-pace effort? No\\\\n\\\\n## Trends\\\\n\\\\n- **Easy pace (≤Z2, list/detail approx.)**: 9:54/mi → 9:50/mi → 9:50/mi → 9:45/mi ▁▃▃█\\\\n\\\\n- **Avg easy HR**: 143 → 144 → 144 → 144 █▁▁▁\\\\n\\\\n- **Long-run distance** (max long-typed, else max run in week): 10.07 mi → 9.89 mi → 10.80 mi → 12.08 mi ▁▁▃█\\\\n\\\\n## Notable\\\\n\\\\n- Relative effort (289) ≥ 1.3× 3-wk avg (217) — possible overload\\\\n\\\\n## Subjective recap\\\\n\\\\nSleep avg this week: 7.1 hrs (range 6.3–7.8)\\\\n\\\\nStrength sessions completed: 1 of 2 (Skipped Friday, travelled for work.)\\\\n\\\\nStress level: Moderate. Normal work week.\\\\n\\\\nBody check: Fine. Legs a bit heavy on Thursday after the tempo.\\\\n\\\\nGoing into next week feeling: Keen. The heat is the main thing, it was 25 degrees at 6 am by Sunday.\\\\n\\\\n## Questions for coach\\\\n\\\\n1. Is 3:30 realistic for me from where I am now?\\\\n\\"}],\\"recentHistoryLimit\\":4,\\"planWeek\\":{\\"isoWeek\\":\\"2026-W36\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-08-31T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-09-07T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-08-31\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-09-06\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgTqhJeHxfyPvNVAa5UhM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Given last week's down week of 33.7 miles and a 10.86 long run, with the athlete feeling great and the marathon 11 weeks away on Nov 15, it's time to build back toward the previous peak of 15.17 for the long run. This week I'll aim for roughly 40 miles total, a 16-mile long run, and a quality session with marathon pace work, plus address the athlete's fueling question for long runs, using Mon/Fri as fixed rest days across Aug 31–Sep 6.\\n\\nI'm sketching the week: easy 6 Tuesday, easy 7-8 Wednesday, a quality MP session Thursday (roughly 2mi warmup, 2x3mi at MP with jog recovery, cooldown, totaling ~9 miles), easy Saturday, and a 16-mile long run Sunday with the last 3 at MP. Adding it up comes to around 42-45 miles, so I'm trimming slightly to about 42 since this follows a cutback week.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WLgJMPBr8kfkXZXWndfnha","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"analysis":"## Why this shape\\n\\nLast week was a planned step back and you came out of it feeling bouncy, so this week rebuilds to around 42 miles with the long run going to 16 and one quality session touching marathon pace. You are 11 weeks out from Lakeshore, which is the right window to start layering goal-pace work into the long run instead of only doing it on Thursday.\\n\\n## The quality day\\n\\nThursday is marathon-pace reps: 2 x 3 miles at 8:00/mi with 3 minutes easy jog between. Two three-mile blocks are long enough to feel like race rhythm but short enough that you are not hanging on in the second half, which is where pacing errors show up. If 8:00 feels forced in the first rep, settle at 8:05 and we will tighten it over the next few weeks.\\n\\n## The long run\\n\\nSixteen miles Sunday, mostly steady, with the last 3 miles lifted to marathon-pace effort. This is the first time this block we are putting goal pace on tired legs, and it is the single most useful workout between here and taper. Start the first 10 miles honest and easy (9:40 – 9:55), then roll into 8:00 – 8:05 for the finish.\\n\\n## Easy running and context\\n\\nEasy pace has crept from 9:50 to 9:34 at the same 144 HR, which is exactly the aerobic drift you want. Keep Tue, Wed and Sat properly easy — if HR climbs toward 150 early, slow down. Fueling for the long runs matters more now that we are over two hours, and I have put specifics in the answer.","headline":"You came out of the cutback week fresh, so this week rebuilds to about 42 miles with a 16-mile long run that finishes at marathon pace — the first real goal-pace work on tired legs.","flags":[{"title":"Long-run drift","detail":"Sunday's HR drifted +8.5 bpm over 10.86 mi. Start the 16-miler conservatively so the MP finish is clean, not a grind."},{"title":"First MP-on-tired-legs","detail":"Last 3 mi of Sunday at 8:00 – 8:05/mi is a new stress. If it falls apart, that is useful data, not a problem."},{"title":"Fueling plan","detail":"Over two hours of running now needs carbs and fluid in. Details in the answer."},{"title":"Shin history","detail":"Jump from 33.7 to ~42 mi is in bounds but at the top of safe. Any shin tightness, flag it immediately."}],"answers":[{"question":"What should I be eating and drinking on the long runs? I've mostly gone without so far.","answer":"Short version: once a long run goes over about 75 minutes, you want roughly 30 – 60 g of carbohydrate per hour and fluid to thirst, plus a small amount of sodium if it is warm.\\n\\nFor Sunday's 16-miler, a workable plan looks like this:\\n\\n| When | What |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 30 – 60 min before | Small carb snack: banana, toast with honey, or half a bagel. Water. |\\n| Every 25 – 30 min on the run | One gel (~22 – 25 g carbs) with a few sips of water |\\n| Fluid | 400 – 600 ml/hr sipped, more if humid. Carry a handheld or soft flask. |\\n| Sodium | If sweating heavily, an electrolyte tab in the bottle (~300 – 500 mg sodium/hr) |\\n\\nPractical notes. Take the first gel at 25 – 30 minutes, not when you feel flat — by then it is too late. Practise with the exact gel brand and flavour you plan to use on race day, because marathon day is not the time to find out your stomach hates a flavour. Maurten, SIS and Gu are all fine; pick one and stick with it for the block.\\n\\nFor Thursday's workout you do not need fuel on the run, but eat something small 30 – 60 min before so you can actually hit pace.\\n\\nIf anything new causes stomach trouble, back off that product and try another — this is a coaching question, not a medical one, but if gut issues persist talk to a sports dietitian."}],"prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W36","days":[{"date":"2026-08-31","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest with travel. Walk, hydrate, early night before Tuesday."},{"date":"2026-09-01","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy opener. HR under 148, pace is whatever that gives you.","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:35 – 9:50/mi, HR 135 – 148","distanceM":9656,"structure":"6 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-02","dayType":"easy","notes":"Midweek aerobic run before Thursday's quality. Nothing creeping into Z3.","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:35 – 9:50/mi, HR under 150","distanceM":11265,"structure":"7 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-03","dayType":"quality","notes":"Marathon-pace reps. First real touch of 8:00/mi this block — settle in, do not force it.","run":{"type":"quality","shoes":"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4","paceOrHr":"MP 8:00/mi, recoveries ~10:00/mi","distanceM":12875,"structure":"2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, 2 x 3 mi @ 8:00/mi w/ 3 min easy jog, 1 mi CD"}},{"date":"2026-09-04","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest. Travel day. Eat and sleep well for Sunday."},{"date":"2026-09-05","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy, no chasing.","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148","distanceM":8047,"structure":"5 mi easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-06","dayType":"long","notes":"16 mi with last 3 at MP. First MP-on-tired-legs rep. Start honest, finish strong — fuel plan in the answer.","run":{"type":"long","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"First 13 mi 9:40 – 9:55/mi, last 3 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi","distanceM":25750,"structure":"13 mi steady easy, then 3 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi (no CD needed)"}}]}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":18788,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2736,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard","output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":317}},"diagnostics":null}	200	44923	tool_use	18788	2736	0	0	ok	\N	317
f1c854ad-56ea-41a5-9faa-7e4f66ccae01	2026-09-06 20:00:38.015203+00	weekly_reply	anthropic	claude-opus-4-7	dev	ba71dfa0-e641-49e5-ab16-05476f66eca8	\N	{"model": "claude-opus-4-7", "tools": [{"name": "submit_weekly_reply", "description": "Submit the weekly coaching analysis and structured 7-day prescribed week.", "input_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["headline", "analysis", "prescribedWeek"], "properties": {"flags": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "detail"], "properties": {"title": {"type": "string", "description": "A few words, at most 80 characters."}, "detail": {"type": "string", "description": "One line, at most 280 characters."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "What you are watching, not a summary of the week. Shown as a short list under \\"Watching\\"."}, "answers": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["question", "answer"], "properties": {"answer": {"type": "string", "description": "Your answer, in markdown if it needs more than a paragraph."}, "question": {"type": "string", "description": "The athlete's question, quoted exactly as they wrote it."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 8, "description": "One per question the athlete asked in the check-in or the conversation. Empty when they asked nothing."}, "analysis": {"type": "string", "description": "The rest of your read in markdown: your reasoning about the week and the plan. The athlete sees the headline, flags and answers above it."}, "headline": {"type": "string", "description": "One or two plain sentences, at most 280 characters: what you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan. No markdown, no label."}, "prescribedWeek": {"type": "object", "required": ["week", "days"], "properties": {"days": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["date", "dayType"], "properties": {"run": {"type": ["object", "null"], "required": ["type", "distanceM"], "properties": {"type": {"enum": ["easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "shoes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "paceOrHr": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "distanceM": {"type": "number"}, "structure": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "date": {"type": "string", "description": "Local calendar date YYYY-MM-DD"}, "notes": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Why this session, this week: one or two sentences, under 200 characters. Shown beside the session."}, "dayType": {"enum": ["rest", "easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "strength": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 7, "minItems": 7}, "week": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO week id, e.g. 2026-W43"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "safetyCallouts": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "body"], "properties": {"body": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 600, "description": "At most 600 characters. One or two short paragraphs; the reply carries the detail."}, "title": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 120}, "severity": {"enum": ["info", "caution", "urgent"], "type": "string"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "Visible safety callouts when red flags appear — escalate to professionals; do not diagnose."}, "settingsProposalBullets": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Optional soft-gate dossier/settings proposal bullets (short)."}}, "additionalProperties": false}}], "stream": true, "system": "You are Coach Cade, a running coach producing a weekly training plan. You are Coach Cade, a running coach. Use only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative. Do not sign off with your name or refer to yourself by name or in the third person unless the athlete asks who you are. Speak to the athlete in plain running language. Never use internal terms such as dossier, proposal, soft-gate, packet, recap, subjective, artifact, RPE, mid-week or prescribed. Say \\"what I know about you\\", \\"your weekly report\\", \\"your check-in\\", \\"the plan\\", \\"how hard it felt\\" (or \\"effort 7/10\\"), \\"during the week\\" and \\"planned\\" instead, and never tell the athlete to open a page by name. Week ids such as 2026-W43 are internal: never write them or a short form such as W43 or W43's. Name a week the way the athlete reads it: \\"this week\\", \\"next week\\", \\"the week of Oct 19\\", \\"Oct 19 – 25\\" or \\"4 weeks out from the race\\". Write plain sentences. No exclamation marks, no sentences that open with \\"Let's\\", and no emoji or decorative symbols. Write dates as \\"Mon Oct 19\\" and ranges as \\"Oct 19 – 25\\", never as 2026-10-19. Lead with the conclusion: the first sentence says what matters most. Give each paragraph one idea and state it in the paragraph's first sentence. Keep paragraphs to at most three sentences, and sentences under 25 words. When you give more than three numbers that belong together, such as splits, paces or heart rates, put them in a Markdown table instead of a sentence. Keep your reasoning as sentences: say why, not just what. The headline is the sentence you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan: one or two plain sentences, no label. Flags are what you are watching, not a summary of the week: at most five, each a few-word title and one line of detail. Choose flags by what the athlete needs to act on. A correction comes first: when the athlete ran a session differently from the plan, flag it, and explain in the analysis what happened and why it matters. Answers are one per question the athlete asked in the weekly check-in or the conversation, with the question quoted exactly as they wrote it. When they asked nothing, leave answers empty. Put the reason for each session in that day's notes: one or two sentences on why this session, this week, under 200 characters. The athlete sees them beside the session. The athlete reads the headline, then the flags, then the answers, then the analysis, and the analysis may be folded away. Make each flag and answer stand on its own: never point to something \\"above\\" or \\"below\\", or to a table in another part. The analysis holds your reasoning. Do not restate the headline, flags or answers in it, and never repeat an answer's table or list there. Do not list the days or describe each session in it, as a list or as a table; the day rows already show them. Safety callouts: - Never diagnose injury, illness, or medical conditions; do not prescribe treatment. - Coach running from the athlete's volunteered context and Tempo-derived training data. - When red flags appear (pain that worsens, chest pain, dizziness, sudden injury, illness), emit safetyCallouts and urge a qualified professional. - Each callout needs a short title, athlete-facing body (escalate, do not diagnose), and optional severity (info|caution|urgent). - Keep each callout body under 600 characters: one or two short paragraphs. - Keep the training plan/reply conservative when callouts are present. Think it through first, then call the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer. Do not write the answer as plain text.", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reviewed week (already happened): 2026-W36\\nPlan week ISO label: 2026-W37\\nPlan week Monday (YYYY-MM-DD): 2026-09-07\\nprescribedWeek.days must use these exact dates Mon–Sun: 2026-09-07, 2026-09-08, 2026-09-09, 2026-09-10, 2026-09-11, 2026-09-12, 2026-09-13\\nPrescribe one coherent week of running training for this athlete.\\nCall the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer.\\nInclude a headline (the one or two sentences you would say if you could say only one thing), flags (what you are watching), answers (one per question the athlete asked, quoted as written) and the analysis (your reasoning).\\nWhen red flags appear, populate safetyCallouts (escalate; do not diagnose).\\nModel packet (redacted; no identity PII or GPS):\\n{\\"dossier\\":{\\"versionId\\":1,\\"body\\":\\"# About you\\\\n\\\\n## Stance\\\\nUse only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative.\\\\n\\\\n## Goal\\\\nYou are training for the Lakeshore Marathon on Sun Nov 15, aiming for 3:30. This will be your fourth marathon, and your personal best is 3:41 from two years ago.\\\\n\\\\n## Schedule\\\\nYou run five days a week: Tue, Wed, Thu, Sat and Sun. Mon and Fri are fixed rest days because of work travel.\\\\n\\\\nWeekday runs are early, around 6am before work, capped near an hour. Saturday is an easy hour and Sunday is the long run.\\\\n\\\\nTypical volume is 6 to 7 hours a week, with more on long-run weeks.\\\\n\\\\n## Running history\\\\nYou have run three marathons already, best 3:41. You eased off in June and have built back to about 30 miles a week through July.\\\\n\\\\nRecent weeks show four easy runs of roughly 5 to 6 miles and a long run of about 10.8 miles on Sun Aug 2.\\\\n\\\\n## Injuries and constraints\\\\nYou are healthy right now. You had shin splints in your first marathon block, so you prefer to ramp mileage up carefully.\\\\n\\\\nWork travel takes you away about one week in six. Mon and Fri rest days cannot move.\\\\n\\\\n## Preferences\\\\nYou train mostly on flat paved loops and a canal path, with one hillier route on Thursdays. You want easy days to be properly easy and you are happy to work hard on quality days.\\\\n\\\\nYou like to know why a session is in the plan. Shoes: Brooks Ghost 16 for daily miles, Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 for workouts.\\\\n\\\\n## Current status\\\\nYou are at about 30 miles a week with a long run near 11 miles. Units are miles. Heart rate zones are set custom, with easy running in 112 – 150 bpm.\\"},\\"recapMarkdown\\":\\"# Weekly Recap — 2026-W36 (Mon Aug 31 – Sun Sep 6, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Summary\\\\n\\\\n| Metric | This week | Prev week | 3-wk avg | Δ |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Mileage | 44.41 mi | 33.72 mi | 38.00 mi | +6.4 |\\\\n| Runs | 5 | 5 | 5.0 | +0.0 |\\\\n| Total time | 6h 49m | 5h 18m | 6h 0m | +49m |\\\\n| Total elevation | 955 ft | 587 ft | 773 ft | +182 ft |\\\\n| Relative effort | 328 | 271 | 306 | +22 |\\\\n| Avg easy-run HR | 144 | 144 | 144 | +0 |\\\\n\\\\n## HR zone distribution (week total)\\\\n\\\\nZ1 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  1.2%   (5m)\\\\nZ2 ██████████████████████████░░░░░░░░░  73.4%   (5h 1m)\\\\nZ3 ████████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  22.6%   (1h 33m)\\\\nZ4 █░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  2.8%   (11m)\\\\nZ5 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  0%   (0m)\\\\n\\\\nTarget for marathon base phase: **≥80% in Z1+Z2**. This week: **74.64%**. **Below** typical marathon base target (≥80% Z1+Z2).\\\\n\\\\n## Run-by-run\\\\n\\\\n### Tue Sep 1 — Easy Run — 5.91 mi / 56m 22s\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:33/mi  ·  Avg HR 142 (75.5% max)  ·  Max HR 147  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:30/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:28/mi / 9:31/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:26/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 139.2 → 144 (+4.7 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 1.8% · Z2 98.2% · Z3 0% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑121 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 39\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 3/10  ·  Felt: 8/10\\\\n\\\\n### Wed Sep 2 — Easy Run — 9.18 mi / 1h 26m\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:23/mi  ·  Avg HR 149 (79.3% max)  ·  Max HR 154  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:26/mi / 9:22/mi / 9:22/mi / 9:20/mi / 9:22/mi / 9:20/mi / 9:20/mi / 9:22/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:25/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 146.3 → 151 (+4.7 bpm)\\\\n\\