\\\\n\\\\n## Questions for coach\\\\n\\\\n1. Is 3:30 realistic for me from where I am now?\\\\n\\"}],\\"recentHistoryLimit\\":4,\\"planWeek\\":{\\"isoWeek\\":\\"2026-W37\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-09-07T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-09-14T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-09-07\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-09-13\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgTqm4fn6TwZvJ3zaqCkV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"That jump from 33.7 to 44.4 miles is a steep 31% increase, and with the long run showing significant HR drift, this week really calls for a cutback. With 10 weeks until the November 15 race, I'm planning the September 7-13 week as a modest reduction in volume.\\n\\nI'm laying out roughly 36-38 miles with a 13-14 mile long run (no marathon pace finish this time), Thursday quality work with mile repeats at marathon pace, and I want to build in gel practice during the long run since fueling rehearsal matters. Rest Monday and Friday, easy days filling the gaps, totaling about 38 miles overall—a solid step down from the previous 44.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K4jQPnrLcFd4Z3nHRFTT6T","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"headline":"Big jump last week landed well, so this week is a planned step down to let the gains stick before we push the long run past 16 again. Marathon pace at 8:00/mi is now a real tool, not a hope.","analysis":"## Why a cutback now\\n\\nYou went from 33.7 miles to 44.4 in a single step, with a 16-mile long run that finished at marathon pace. That is a lot of new load in one week, and the +13.4 bpm drift on Sunday tells me the back half cost more than the pace suggests. A small pullback now protects the gains and keeps the shins quiet.\\n\\n## What I like\\n\\nMarathon pace on Thursday sat at 7:58 – 8:01/mi with an average HR of 153 and you called it a 6/10. That is the first clean read we have had on goal pace, and the fact that you felt controlled is the sentence I care about most. Easy pace has drifted from 9:43 to 9:30 at the same 144 HR, which is honest aerobic progress.\\n\\n## What this week does\\n\\nMileage lands near 38 with the long run at 14 and no marathon-pace finish. Thursday is four MP miles broken into reps so you touch 8:00/mi again without stacking two hard long efforts back to back. If next Sunday feels crisp, the week after we go back up — likely 16 again, this time with a longer MP block built in.\\n\\n## Easy running\\n\\nThe two easy days drifted into Z3 last week (Wed 19% Z3, long run 19% Z3). That is where cutback weeks earn their keep. Start the first mile at 10:00/mi or slower on every easy day and let HR, not pace, set the ceiling.","flags":[{"title":"Big mileage jump","detail":"44.4 mi was +31% on the prior week. Pulling back to ~38 this week to consolidate before pushing again."},{"title":"Long-run HR drift","detail":"Sunday drifted +13.4 bpm, above the ≤10 target. Partly the MP finish, partly heat — watching this on the next long run."},{"title":"Easy days creeping","detail":"Wed easy run and the long run both sat ~19% in Z3. Easy means under 150 bpm, pace is whatever that is."},{"title":"Z1+Z2 at 74.6%","detail":"Below the 80% base target. Natural consequence of the MP work, but worth keeping an eye on."}],"answers":[{"question":"Is it worth trying a gel on the long run next week, or keep it simple?","answer":"Yes, start practising now. Take one gel around 45 minutes and a second around 90 minutes on Sunday's 14. The point is not fuel need on a 14-miler — it is teaching your stomach to take sugar while running, so it is a non-event on race day when you will need four or five of them. Use whatever brand you plan to carry at Lakeshore and wash it down with a few sips of water or sports drink."}],"prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W37","days":[{"date":"2026-09-07","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest with travel. Short walk and hip mobility if you have ten minutes."},{"date":"2026-09-08","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy opener after a big week. First mile at 10:00/mi — do not chase pace, let HR settle.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","structure":"5 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-09","dayType":"easy","notes":"Standard midweek aerobic. Keep this one in Z2 — it slid into Z3 last Wednesday.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":11265,"paceOrHr":"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","structure":"7 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-10","dayType":"quality","notes":"MP reps with short recoveries. Touch 8:00/mi again without stacking a second long MP block on Sunday.","run":{"type":"quality","distanceM":11265,"paceOrHr":"MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 9:45 – 10:00/mi","shoes":"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4","structure":"2 mi WU · 4 x 1 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"}},{"date":"2026-09-11","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest. Good day for the second strength session if you are home."},{"date":"2026-09-12","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat, cut to 4 — no hero stuff.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"9:50 – 10:00/mi","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","structure":"5 mi steady easy"}},{"date":"2026-09-13","dayType":"long","notes":"Steady 14, no MP finish. Fuel at 45 and 90 minutes to start building the race-day habit.","run":{"type":"long","distanceM":22531,"paceOrHr":"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 152","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","structure":"14 mi steady. One gel at ~45 min, one at ~90 min, with fluid."}}]}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":23060,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_