Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑420 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 103\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 7/10  ·  Felt: 5/10\\\\n\\\\n### Long run — Sun Aug 23 — 15.17 mi / 2h 26m\\\\n\\\\nAvg pace: 9:41/mi  ·  Avg HR 148  ·  Drift: 144 → 151.3 (+7.3 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:26/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:43/mi / 9:36/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:38/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:39/mi / 9:33/mi\\\\n\\\\nBack half avg pace: 9:36/mi vs front 9:35/mi (vs front half; Δ -1s/mi)\\\\n\\\\nLast 3 mi at marathon-pace effort? No\\\\n\\\\n## Trends\\\\n\\\\n- **Easy pace (≤Z2, list/detail approx.)**: 9:50/mi → 9:45/mi → 9:43/mi → 9:41/mi ▁▃▅█ (improving)\\\\n\\\\n- **Avg easy HR**: 144 → 144 → 144 → 144 ▅▅▅▅\\\\n\\\\n- **Long-run distance** (max long-typed, else max run in week): 10.80 mi → 12.08 mi → 13.03 mi → 15.17 mi ▁▃▅█ (improving)\\\\n\\\\n## Subjective recap\\\\n\\\\nSleep avg this week: 6.4 hrs (range 5.5–7.2)\\\\n\\\\nStrength sessions completed: 1 of 2 (Too tired Wednesday evening.)\\\\n\\\\nStress level: High. Deadline at work, late nights Tuesday and Wednesday.\\\\n\\\\nBody check: Tired more than sore. Saturday's easy run felt like work.\\\\n\\\\nGoing into next week feeling: Hoping the next week eases off a bit.\\\\n\\\\n## Questions for coach\\\\n\\\\n1. My schedule gets tight when I travel for work. How should I handle missed runs?\\\\n\\",\\"analysis\\":\\"## Why this week looks the way it does\\\\n\\\\nYou handled 37.7 miles with an honest long run and the aerobic signal is clean, so the next step is a small build to about 40 with one controlled tempo. Easy HR has stayed at 144 while easy pace nudged from 9:50 to 9:43, which is exactly the direction you want for a marathon base.\\\\n\\\\nThursday is a tempo rather than reps because you flagged the 800s felt too fast early. Running sustained 4 miles near 8:15 – 8:25 per mile teaches pacing discipline and gives a cleaner read on where fitness sits versus 3:30 goal pace of 8:00/mi. The Endorphin Speed 4 is the right shoe for it.\\\\n\\\\nThe long run grows to 14 miles at the same easy effort you ran Sunday. If the air is thick at 6am again, drop pace by 10 – 20 seconds per mile from the start rather than hanging on and drifting late, which is what the +6.9 bpm drift on Sunday hinted at.\\\\n\\\\nMileage is up roughly 2 miles on last week, well inside a safe ramp given your shin history. Keep the two strength sessions in and the sleep closer to 7.5 if you can; it is the cheapest gain available right now.\\",\\"prescribedWeek\\":{\\"days\\":[{\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-17\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Fixed rest day. Travel day, nothing to prove.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"rest\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 135 – 148\\",\\"distanceM\\":8047,\\"structure\\":\\"5 mi steady easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-18\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Easy shakeout to open the week. Keep HR under 150 and let pace be whatever that is.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 135 – 148\\",\\"distanceM\\":12875,\\"structure\\":\\"8 mi steady easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-19\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Standard midweek aerobic run before Thursday's tempo. Nothing creeping into Z3.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"quality\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"Tempo miles 8:15 – 8:25/mi\\",\\"distanceM\\":11265,\\"structure\\":\\"2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, 4 mi tempo @ 8:15 – 8:25/mi (one continuous block), 1 mi CD\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-20\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Continuous tempo to practice pacing discipline after last week's fast 800s. Steady is the win here.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"quality\\"},{\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-21\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Fixed rest day. Full recovery before the weekend double.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"rest\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148\\",\\"distanceM\\":9656,\\"structure\\":\\"6 mi easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-22\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy, no pace chasing.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"long\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 142 – 150\\",\\"distanceM\\":22531,\\"structure\\":\\"14 mi continuous easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-23\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Long run up to 14 mi at the same easy effort as last Sunday. If humid at 6am, start 10 – 20 sec/mi slower.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"long\\"}],\\"week\\":\\"2026-W34\\"}}],\\"recentHistoryLimit\\":4,\\"planWeek\\":{\\"isoWeek\\":\\"2026-W39\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-09-21T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-09-28T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-09-21\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-09-27\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgTqtHn1FDUVBzCVQM2xt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Since the athlete is recovering from Achilles trouble and only cleared for one short tempo, I'm rebuilding the week cautiously—starting with rest Monday, then easy runs Tuesday and Wednesday before introducing anything more demanding later in the week, keeping total volume modest given last week's 26 miles.\\n\\nFor Thursday I'm giving the approved short tempo with a generous warm-up and cooldown, then rest Friday, an easy 5 Saturday, and a long run of 11-12 miles Sunday to rebuild endurance gradually. On the goal question, I think we hold the 3:30 target for now since the earlier 16-mile plus marathon-pace workout showed real capacity, but we'll need to reassess based on how the next few weeks of Achilles recovery go.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JtyGXPJEnBAgzgMC2UbbHH","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"headline":"Achilles has settled and the physio has cleared a short tempo, so this week we rebuild carefully: steady easy miles, a 2-mile tempo on Thursday, and a 12-mile long run.","analysis":"## Where we are\\n\\nYou lost about a week of load but the aerobic base is intact. Easy pace is still 9:20 – 9:30/mi at HR 142 – 143, and the Sunday long run drifted a reasonable +8 bpm over 8 miles. That is a healthy platform to build from with eight weeks until Lakeshore.\\n\\n## Why this shape\\n\\nThe physio cleared one short tempo, so Thursday is deliberately small: a 2-mile tempo at 8:15 – 8:25/mi, not marathon pace. Short enough to test the Achilles under load, long enough to feel like a workout. If morning stiffness returns at any point before Thursday, swap it for an easy 5.\\n\\nThe long run moves back to 12 miles at pure easy effort. No marathon pace finish this week — we need two clean weeks of tempo and long run before layering goal pace on tired legs again. If Sunday goes well, the week of Sep 28 goes to 14 and we start reintroducing MP work the week after.\\n\\n## Easy running\\n\\nThree of the four easy runs last week crept toward 9:17 – 9:20/mi. That is sharper than it needs to be in a rebuild week. Open every run at 10:00/mi for the first mile and let HR, not pace, decide the rest.\\n\\n## Keep doing\\n\\nTwice-daily heel drops, the two short strength sessions, and the pre-run stiffness check. If first-mile stiffness ever rises above a 3/10 or sharpens with pace, stop the run and tell me — that is the signal the physio gave you, and it is the right one.","flags":[{"title":"Achilles check before every run","detail":"Morning stiffness should stay gone. Any sharpening with pace means stop and reassess, especially Thursday."},{"title":"Easy pace drifting fast","detail":"Easy runs sat at 9:17 – 9:23/mi last week. Open at 10:00/mi and let HR cap the rest, not pace."},{"title":"Tempo is a test, not a workout","detail":"Thursday's 2 miles is to see how the Achilles handles sustained effort. Hold 8:15 – 8:25/mi — do not drift toward MP."},{"title":"Long run back to easy only","detail":"12 mi steady, no MP finish. We need two clean weeks before putting goal pace on tired legs again."}],"answers":[{"question":"The physio is happy for me to try a short tempo next week. Can we put one in?","answer":"Yes, Thursday. I have kept it small on purpose: 2 mi warm-up, 2 mi tempo at 8:15 – 8:25/mi, 1 mi cool-down, in the Endorphin Speed 4.\\n\\nTwo things to respect. One, this is a tempo, not marathon pace — do not let it drift toward 8:00/mi because the shoe feels fast. Two, if first-mile stiffness is above a 2/10 or the Achilles sharpens as you lift the pace, stop and jog home easy. That is exactly what the physio told you and it is the right rule."},{"question":"Does the missed week change what is realistic for race day?","answer":"Not yet. 3:30 is still on the table and I am not moving off it this week.\\n\\nHere is the honest read. You lost about one week of load, not fitness — easy pace and HR are unchanged, and the 16-mile long run with MP finish on Sep 6 showed the engine is there. Eight weeks is enough to rebuild if the Achilles stays quiet.\\n\\nThe decision point is the week of Oct 12, roughly four weeks out. By then we need to have put together one 18 – 20 mile long run with a solid MP block and a longer MP session midweek. If the Achilles cooperates and those land, 3:30 stays the plan. If we lose more time, we talk about either a slightly softer goal (3:35) or a run-by-feel race with 3:30 as a stretch. I will flag it clearly when we get there — you will not be guessing."}],"prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W39","days":[{"date":"2026-09-21","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest with travel. Heel drops morning and evening."},{"date":"2026-09-22","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy opener. First mile at 10:00/mi — check stiffness as you go, let HR settle before finding rhythm.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148","structure":"5 mi steady easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"}},{"date":"2026-09-23","dayType":"easy","notes":"Standard midweek aerobic. Keep it honest in Z2 — this run crept to 15% Z3 last week.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":9656,"paceOrHr":"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150","structure":"6 mi steady easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"}},{"date":"2026-09-24","dayType":"quality","notes":"First tempo back. Small on purpose — 8:15 – 8:25/mi, not MP. Stop if the Achilles sharpens with pace.","run":{"type":"quality","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"Tempo 8:15 – 8:25/mi, WU/CD ~9:45/mi","structure":"2 mi WU, 2 mi tempo @ 8:15 – 8:25/mi, 1 mi CD","shoes":"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4"}},{"date":"2026-09-25","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest. Second strength session if you are home."},{"date":"2026-09-26","dayType":"easy","notes":"Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat after Thursday, drop to 4 miles — no hero stuff.","run":{"type":"easy","distanceM":8047,"paceOrHr":"9:50 – 10:00/mi","structure":"5 mi steady easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"}},{"date":"2026-09-27","dayType":"long","notes":"Steady 12, no MP finish. Fuel at ~45 and ~90 minutes with water, keep HR under 152 through mile 8.","run":{"type":"long","distanceM":19312,"paceOrHr":"9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 152","structure":"12 mi continuous easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16"}}]}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":24718,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2746,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard","output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":350}},"diagnostics":null}	200	40403	tool_use	24718	2746	0	0	ok	\N	350
24f57e80-3174-4e7b-a85f-87d8225b18a3	2026-09-27 20:00:44.16432+00	weekly_reply	anthropic	claude-opus-4-7	dev	2453cc65-d713-4a3d-b143-23203b4e72e5	\N	{"model": "claude-opus-4-7", "tools": [{"name": "submit_weekly_reply", "description": "Submit the weekly coaching analysis and structured 7-day prescribed week.", "input_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["headline", "analysis", "prescribedWeek"], "properties": {"flags": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "detail"], "properties": {"title": {"type": "string", "description": "A few words, at most 80 characters."}, "detail": {"type": "string", "description": "One line, at most 280 characters."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "What you are watching, not a summary of the week. Shown as a short list under \\"Watching\\"."}, "answers": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["question", "answer"], "properties": {"answer": {"type": "string", "description": "Your answer, in markdown if it needs more than a paragraph."}, "question": {"type": "string", "description": "The athlete's question, quoted exactly as they wrote it."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 8, "description": "One per question the athlete asked in the check-in or the conversation. Empty when they asked nothing."}, "analysis": {"type": "string", "description": "The rest of your read in markdown: your reasoning about the week and the plan. The athlete sees the headline, flags and answers above it."}, "headline": {"type": "string", "description": "One or two plain sentences, at most 280 characters: what you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan. No markdown, no label."}, "prescribedWeek": {"type": "object", "required": ["week", "days"], "properties": {"days": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["date", "dayType"], "properties": {"run": {"type": ["object", "null"], "required": ["type", "distanceM"], "properties": {"type": {"enum": ["easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "shoes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "paceOrHr": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "distanceM": {"type": "number"}, "structure": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "date": {"type": "string", "description": "Local calendar date YYYY-MM-DD"}, "notes": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Why this session, this week: one or two sentences, under 200 characters. Shown beside the session."}, "dayType": {"enum": ["rest", "easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "strength": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 7, "minItems": 7}, "week": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO week id, e.g. 2026-W43"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "safetyCallouts": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "body"], "properties": {"body": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 600, "description": "At most 600 characters. One or two short paragraphs; the reply carries the detail."}, "title": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 120}, "severity": {"enum": ["info", "caution", "urgent"], "type": "string"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "Visible safety callouts when red flags appear — escalate to professionals; do not diagnose."}, "settingsProposalBullets": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Optional soft-gate dossier/settings proposal bullets (short)."}}, "additionalProperties": false}}], "stream": true, "system": "You are Coach Cade, a running coach producing a weekly training plan. You are Coach Cade, a running coach. Use only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative. Do not sign off with your name or refer to yourself by name or in the third person unless the athlete asks who you are. Speak to the athlete in plain running language. Never use internal terms such as dossier, proposal, soft-gate, packet, recap, subjective, artifact, RPE, mid-week or prescribed. Say \\"what I know about you\\", \\"your weekly report\\", \\"your check-in\\", \\"the plan\\", \\"how hard it felt\\" (or \\"effort 7/10\\"), \\"during the week\\" and \\"planned\\" instead, and never tell the athlete to open a page by name. Week ids such as 2026-W43 are internal: never write them or a short form such as W43 or W43's. Name a week the way the athlete reads it: \\"this week\\", \\"next week\\", \\"the week of Oct 19\\", \\"Oct 19 – 25\\" or \\"4 weeks out from the race\\". Write plain sentences. No exclamation marks, no sentences that open with \\"Let's\\", and no emoji or decorative symbols. Write dates as \\"Mon Oct 19\\" and ranges as \\"Oct 19 – 25\\", never as 2026-10-19. Lead with the conclusion: the first sentence says what matters most. Give each paragraph one idea and state it in the paragraph's first sentence. Keep paragraphs to at most three sentences, and sentences under 25 words. When you give more than three numbers that belong together, such as splits, paces or heart rates, put them in a Markdown table instead of a sentence. Keep your reasoning as sentences: say why, not just what. The headline is the sentence you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan: one or two plain sentences, no label. Flags are what you are watching, not a summary of the week: at most five, each a few-word title and one line of detail. Choose flags by what the athlete needs to act on. A correction comes first: when the athlete ran a session differently from the plan, flag it, and explain in the analysis what happened and why it matters. Answers are one per question the athlete asked in the weekly check-in or the conversation, with the question quoted exactly as they wrote it. When they asked nothing, leave answers empty. Put the reason for each session in that day's notes: one or two sentences on why this session, this week, under 200 characters. The athlete sees them beside the session. The athlete reads the headline, then the flags, then the answers, then the analysis, and the analysis may be folded away. Make each flag and answer stand on its own: never point to something \\"above\\" or \\"below\\", or to a table in another part. The analysis holds your reasoning. Do not restate the headline, flags or answers in it, and never repeat an answer's table or list there. Do not list the days or describe each session in it, as a list or as a table; the day rows already show them. Safety callouts: - Never diagnose injury, illness, or medical conditions; do not prescribe treatment. - Coach running from the athlete's volunteered context and Tempo-derived training data. - When red flags appear (pain that worsens, chest pain, dizziness, sudden injury, illness), emit safetyCallouts and urge a qualified professional. - Each callout needs a short title, athlete-facing body (escalate, do not diagnose), and optional severity (info|caution|urgent). - Keep each callout body under 600 characters: one or two short paragraphs. - Keep the training plan/reply conservative when callouts are present. Think it through first, then call the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer. Do not write the answer as plain text.", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reviewed week (already happened): 2026-W39\\nPlan week ISO label: 2026-W40\\nPlan week Monday (YYYY-MM-DD): 2026-09-28\\nprescribedWeek.days must use these exact dates Mon–Sun: 2026-09-28, 2026-09-29, 2026-09-30, 2026-10-01, 2026-10-02, 2026-10-03, 2026-10-04\\nPrescribe one coherent week of running training for this athlete.\\nCall the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer.\\nInclude a headline (the one or two sentences you would say if you could say only one thing), flags (what you are watching), answers (one per question the athlete asked, quoted as written) and the analysis (your reasoning).\\nWhen red flags appear, populate safetyCallouts (escalate; do not diagnose).\\nModel packet (redacted; no identity PII or GPS):\\n{\\"dossier\\":{\\"versionId\\":1,\\"body\\":\\"# About you\\\\n\\\\n## Stance\\\\nUse only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative.\\\\n\\\\n## Goal\\\\nYou are training for the Lakeshore Marathon on Sun Nov 15, aiming for 3:30. This will be your fourth marathon, and your personal best is 3:41 from two years ago.\\\\n\\\\n## Schedule\\\\nYou run five days a week: Tue, Wed, Thu, Sat and Sun. Mon and Fri are fixed rest days because of work travel.\\\\n\\\\nWeekday runs are early, around 6am before work, capped near an hour. Saturday is an easy hour and Sunday is the long run.\\\\n\\\\nTypical volume is 6 to 7 hours a week, with more on long-run weeks.\\\\n\\\\n## Running history\\\\nYou have run three marathons already, best 3:41. You eased off in June and have built back to about 30 miles a week through July.\\\\n\\\\nRecent weeks show four easy runs of roughly 5 to 6 miles and a long run of about 10.8 miles on Sun Aug 2.\\\\n\\\\n## Injuries and constraints\\\\nYou are healthy right now. You had shin splints in your first marathon block, so you prefer to ramp mileage up carefully.\\\\n\\\\nWork travel takes you away about one week in six. Mon and Fri rest days cannot move.\\\\n\\\\n## Preferences\\\\nYou train mostly on flat paved loops and a canal path, with one hillier route on Thursdays. You want easy days to be properly easy and you are happy to work hard on quality days.\\\\n\\\\nYou like to know why a session is in the plan. Shoes: Brooks Ghost 16 for daily miles, Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 for workouts.\\\\n\\\\n## Current status\\\\nYou are at about 30 miles a week with a long run near 11 miles. Units are miles. Heart rate zones are set custom, with easy running in 112 – 150 bpm.\\"},\\"recapMarkdown\\":\\"# Weekly Recap — 2026-W39 (Mon Sep 21 – Sun Sep 27, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Summary\\\\n\\\\n| Metric | This week | Prev week | 3-wk avg | Δ |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Mileage | 34.83 mi | 25.95 mi | 33.86 mi | +1.0 |\\\\n| Runs | 5 | 5 | 5.0 | +0.0 |\\\\n| Total time | 5h 20m | 4h 4m | 5h 15m | +5m |\\\\n| Total elevation | 722 ft | 525 ft | 741 ft | -20 ft |\\\\n| Relative effort | 274 | 171 | 241 | +33 |\\\\n| Avg easy-run HR | 144 | 143 | 144 | +0 |\\\\n\\\\n## HR zone distribution (week total)\\\\n\\\\nZ1 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  1.2%   (4m)\\\\nZ2 ████████████████████████████░░░░░░░  80.3%   (4h 17m)\\\\nZ3 ████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  12.1%   (39m)\\\\nZ4 ██░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  6.4%   (21m)\\\\nZ5 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  0%   (0m)\\\\n\\\\nTarget for marathon base phase: **≥80% in Z1+Z2**. This week: **81.51%**. On target for ≥80% Z1+Z2.\\\\n\\\\n## Run-by-run\\\\n\\\\n### Tue Sep 22 — Easy Run — 5.16 mi / 48m 4s\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:18/mi  ·  Avg HR 142 (75.5% max)  ·  Max HR 148  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:17/mi / 9:20/mi / 9:23/mi / 9:20/mi / 9:14/mi / 9:17/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 138.5 → 144.1 (+5.5 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 2.1% · Z2 97.9% · Z3 0% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑102 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 34\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 3/10  ·  Felt: 8/10\\\\n\\\\n### Wed Sep 23 — Easy Run — 6.86 mi / 1h 3m\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:17/mi  ·  Avg HR 148 (78.7% max)  ·  Max HR 154  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:07/mi / 9:07/mi / 9:18/mi / 9:15/mi / 9:15/mi / 9:15/mi / 9:15/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 145.1 → 150.3 (+5.2 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 0% · Z2 86.4% · Z3 13.6% · Z4 0% · Z