ts: 9:30/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:28/mi / 9:23/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:30/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:31/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 143 → 151.6 (+8.5 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 0.5% · Z2 78.4% · Z3 21.2% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑187 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 73\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 5/10  ·  Felt: 8/10\\\\n\\\\n### Long run — Sun Aug 30 — 10.86 mi / 1h 44m\\\\n\\\\nAvg pace: 9:38/mi  ·  Avg HR 148  ·  Drift: 143 → 151.6 (+8.5 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:30/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:28/mi / 9:23/mi / 9:26/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:35/mi / 9:30/mi / 9:33/mi / 9:31/mi\\\\n\\\\nBack half avg pace: 9:31/mi vs front 9:28/mi (vs front half; Δ -3s/mi)\\\\n\\\\nLast 3 mi at marathon-pace effort? No\\\\n\\\\n## Trends\\\\n\\\\n- **Easy pace (≤Z2, list/detail approx.)**: 9:45/mi → 9:43/mi → 9:41/mi → 9:34/mi ▁▃▃█ (improving)\\\\n\\\\n- **Avg easy HR**: 144 → 144 → 144 → 144 ▅▅▅▅\\\\n\\\\n- **Long-run distance** (max long-typed, else max run in week): 12.08 mi → 13.03 mi → 15.17 mi → 10.86 mi ▃▅█▁\\\\n\\\\n## Subjective recap\\\\n\\\\nSleep avg this week: 7.4 hrs (range 6.8–8.1)\\\\n\\\\nStrength sessions completed: 2 of 2\\\\n\\\\nStress level: Low. Deadline done.\\\\n\\\\nBody check: Best I've felt in a month. Legs have some bounce again.\\\\n\\\\nGoing into next week feeling: Ready to go again.\\\\n\\\\n## Questions for coach\\\\n\\\\n1. What should I be eating and drinking on the long runs? I've mostly gone without so far.\\\\n\\",\\"analysis\\":\\"## Why a cutback now\\\\n\\\\nYou jumped 7 miles week over week on 6.4 hours of sleep and high work stress, and Saturday's easy run already felt like work. That is the moment to pull back a notch, not push through. The pattern we want in a build is three up, one down, and you have had three up weeks in a row.\\\\n\\\\n## What stays, what shrinks\\\\n\\\\nLong run holds near 12 miles so you keep the aerobic stimulus but with less total load around it. Thursday's workout drops to three marathon-pace miles with full recoveries — enough to touch 8:00/mi cleanly without the Z4 time you got from the longer tempo. Easy days stay the same length but I want them genuinely easy, HR under 150, pace closer to 9:50 than 9:30.\\\\n\\\\n## Looking ahead\\\\n\\\\nIf this week lands well, next week goes back up toward 40 with a 16-mile long run and a longer quality session. If sleep stays short or anything in the shins whispers, we hold flat another week — the race is still 11 weeks out and the base is there.\\",\\"prescribedWeek\\":{\\"days\\":[{\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-24\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Fixed rest with travel. Short walk and some hip mobility if you have 10 minutes.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"rest\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148\\",\\"distanceM\\":8047,\\"structure\\":\\"5 mi steady easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-25\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Easy opener. First mile deliberately slow — let HR settle before you find a rhythm.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150\\",\\"distanceM\\":11265,\\"structure\\":\\"7 mi steady easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-26\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Standard mid-week easy. Keep it in Z2 — this one has crept into Z3 twice recently.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"quality\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 10:00/mi\\",\\"distanceM\\":9656,\\"structure\\":\\"2 mi WU · 3 x 1 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog · 1 mi CD\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-27\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Short goal-pace reps with full recovery. Enough to feel 8:00/mi without draining the week.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"quality\\"},{\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-28\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Fixed rest. Good day for the second short strength session if you are home.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"rest\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"easy\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:50 – 10:00/mi\\",\\"distanceM\\":8047,\\"structure\\":\\"5 mi steady easy\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-29\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat, cut to 4 miles — no hero stuff.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"easy\\"},{\\"run\\":{\\"type\\":\\"long\\",\\"shoes\\":\\"Brooks Ghost 16\\",\\"paceOrHr\\":\\"9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 152\\",\\"distanceM\\":19312,\\"structure\\":\\"12 mi steady, no MP finish\\"},\\"date\\":\\"2026-08-30\\",\\"notes\\":\\"Steady 12. Start before sunrise, carry fluid, hold HR under 150 through mile 8 then let it climb naturally.\\",\\"dayType\\":\\"long\\"}],\\"week\\":\\"2026-W35\\"}}],\\"recentHistoryLimit\\":4,\\"planWeek\\":{\\"isoWeek\\":\\"2026-W40\\",\\"start\\":\\"2026-09-28T04:00:00.000Z\\",\\"end\\":\\"2026-10-05T03:59:59.999Z\\",\\"startDate\\":\\"2026-09-28\\",\\"endDate\\":\\"2026-10-04\\"}}"}], "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "max_tokens": 64000, "tool_choice": {"type": "auto"}, "output_config": {"effort": "medium"}}	{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CgTqwiiGsgfrZ53UUXYRE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Last week's mileage and recovery look solid, so with the race seven weeks out I'm planning a build phase: pushing the long run toward 14 miles with marathon-pace segments, and setting Thursday as the quality day with MP reps. I'm starting to lay out the week-by-week schedule from the September 28 start date.\\n\\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G68sPQgamJMXey2SMJ7BYe","name":"submit_weekly_reply","input":{"headline":"Clean week back on track — the Achilles stayed quiet through a real tempo, so we build cautiously to around 39 miles with marathon-pace work returning to the long run.","analysis":"## Why this shape\\n\\nLast week was the first full load since the Achilles flare and it held together: tempo at 7:35/mi, long run at 9:18/mi with only +8.8 bpm drift, and no body-check flags. That earns a careful step up, not a leap. Seven weeks to race day means two more build weeks, then a peak block, then taper.\\n\\n## The quality day\\n\\nThursday is 2 x 2 miles at marathon pace with a 3-minute jog between. That is a longer continuous touch of 8:00/mi than the three Thursday tempo miles you ran last week, but still well short of the 8-mile MP session from early September. Stay on the flat canal loop, not the Thursday hills — pace changes and climbs are what loaded the tendon last time.\\n\\n## The long run\\n\\nFourteen miles Sunday with the last 2 at marathon pace. This is the first MP-on-tired-legs rep since the Achilles episode, kept deliberately short so you finish wanting more. If anything whispers in the tendon during the first 10 miles, drop the MP finish and jog it home — no points for toughing it out this far from the race.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\nKeep the heel drops going daily, keep the Ghost 16 for everything except Thursday, and keep easy days honest (HR under 150). Wednesday drifted to 13.6% Z3 last week — fine, but let's keep it Z2 this week with Thursday getting harder.","flags":[{"title":"Achilles maintenance","detail":"Clean week but the tendon is still the limiter. Keep heel drops daily and flag any morning stiffness before it builds."},{"title":"Easy-day creep","detail":"Wednesday slid into Z3 (13.6%) again. Hold it under 150 bpm so Thursday's quality has somewhere to go."},{"title":"MP returns to the long run","detail":"First MP finish since early Sept. Short on purpose — 2 miles, not 3, to re-introduce the stimulus."}],"answers":[{"question":"Is there anything I should keep doing for the Achilles through race day?","answer":"Yes — treat this as ongoing maintenance, not a problem you've closed out.\\n\\n- **Heel drops daily**, both straight-leg and bent-knee, through race day. Two sets of 15 each side is plenty. Your physio prescribed them for a reason; they are the single highest-value thing you can do.\\n- **Morning stiffness is your dashboard.** If the first few steps out of bed feel normal, you are winning. If you notice a limp or sharp pain in the first 20 steps, that is the day to tell me and ease back.\\n- **Shoes:** stay in the Ghost 16 for all easy and long miles. The Speed 4 only comes out for the Thursday workout. Don't add a new shoe between now and the race.\\n- **Hills and surges** are what provoked it. Keep Thursday on the flat canal and skip the hillier Thursday route until after Lakeshore.\\n- **Warm up a little longer** before quality — an extra 5 minutes of easy jogging before you pick up the pace is cheap insurance.\\n- **Race day:** no last-minute shoe swap, no aggressive first mile. The tendon will tolerate your current load; it won't tolerate a surprise.\\n\\nIf anything changes — swelling, a limp, sharper pain under load — check back with your physio before we adjust training."}],"prescribedWeek":{"week":"2026-W40","days":[{"date":"2026-09-28","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest and travel. Heel drops morning and evening."},{"date":"2026-09-29","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:30 – 9:50/mi, HR under 148","distanceM":8047,"structure":"5 mi steady easy on flat pavement"},"notes":"Easy opener. First mile deliberately slow to let the tendon warm through."},{"date":"2026-09-30","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:25 – 9:45/mi, HR under 150","distanceM":11265,"structure":"7 mi steady easy"},"notes":"Midweek aerobic. Keep this in Z2 — it drifted to Z3 last week.","strength":"Short strength: hips, core, slow heel drops."},{"date":"2026-10-01","dayType":"quality","run":{"type":"quality","shoes":"Saucony Endorphin Speed 4","paceOrHr":"MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 9:45/mi","distanceM":12070,"structure":"2 mi WU · 2 x 2 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 3 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"},"notes":"Longer MP touch than last week, still short of September's tempo. Flat canal only — no hills."},{"date":"2026-10-02","dayType":"rest","notes":"Fixed rest, travel. Walk, eat, sleep well for Sunday."},{"date":"2026-10-03","dayType":"easy","run":{"type":"easy","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"9:30 – 9:50/mi","distanceM":6437,"structure":"4 mi easy"},"notes":"Short easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy.","strength":"Second strength session: heel drops, hips, core."},{"date":"2026-10-04","dayType":"long","run":{"type":"long","shoes":"Brooks Ghost 16","paceOrHr":"First 12 mi 9:30 – 9:50/mi, last 2 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi","distanceM":22531,"structure":"12 mi steady easy, then 2 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi"},"notes":"14 mi with a short MP finish — first MP-on-tired-legs since the Achilles. Drop the MP if anything whispers in the first 10."}]}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":24545,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2688,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard","output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":448}},"diagnostics":null}	200	43844	tool_use	24545	2688	0	0	ok	\N	448
10e0afd6-e1c5-430a-a758-ac077b85d938	2026-10-04 20:01:43.157956+00	weekly_reply	anthropic	claude-opus-4-7	dev	8d1621a0-dfc2-47d7-ac36-0c58953ae4b1	\N	{"model": "claude-opus-4-7", "tools": [{"name": "submit_weekly_reply", "description": "Submit the weekly coaching analysis and structured 7-day prescribed week.", "input_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["headline", "analysis", "prescribedWeek"], "properties": {"flags": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "detail"], "properties": {"title": {"type": "string", "description": "A few words, at most 80 characters."}, "detail": {"type": "string", "description": "One line, at most 280 characters."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "What you are watching, not a summary of the week. Shown as a short list under \\"Watching\\"."}, "answers": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["question", "answer"], "properties": {"answer": {"type": "string", "description": "Your answer, in markdown if it needs more than a paragraph."}, "question": {"type": "string", "description": "The athlete's question, quoted exactly as they wrote it."}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 8, "description": "One per question the athlete asked in the check-in or the conversation. Empty when they asked nothing."}, "analysis": {"type": "string", "description": "The rest of your read in markdown: your reasoning about the week and the plan. The athlete sees the headline, flags and answers above it."}, "headline": {"type": "string", "description": "One or two plain sentences, at most 280 characters: what you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan. No markdown, no label."}, "prescribedWeek": {"type": "object", "required": ["week", "days"], "properties": {"days": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["date", "dayType"], "properties": {"run": {"type": ["object", "null"], "required": ["type", "distanceM"], "properties": {"type": {"enum": ["easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "shoes": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "paceOrHr": {"type": ["string", "null"]}, "distanceM": {"type": "number"}, "structure": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "date": {"type": "string", "description": "Local calendar date YYYY-MM-DD"}, "notes": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Why this session, this week: one or two sentences, under 200 characters. Shown beside the session."}, "dayType": {"enum": ["rest", "easy", "quality", "long", "race", "other"], "type": "string"}, "strength": {"type": ["string", "null"]}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 7, "minItems": 7}, "week": {"type": "string", "description": "ISO week id, e.g. 2026-W43"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "safetyCallouts": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["title", "body"], "properties": {"body": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 600, "description": "At most 600 characters. One or two short paragraphs; the reply carries the detail."}, "title": {"type": "string", "maxLength": 120}, "severity": {"enum": ["info", "caution", "urgent"], "type": "string"}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "maxItems": 5, "description": "Visible safety callouts when red flags appear — escalate to professionals; do not diagnose."}, "settingsProposalBullets": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Optional soft-gate dossier/settings proposal bullets (short)."}}, "additionalProperties": false}}], "stream": true, "system": "You are Coach Cade, a running coach producing a weekly training plan. You are Coach Cade, a running coach. Use only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative. Do not sign off with your name or refer to yourself by name or in the third person unless the athlete asks who you are. Speak to the athlete in plain running language. Never use internal terms such as dossier, proposal, soft-gate, packet, recap, subjective, artifact, RPE, mid-week or prescribed. Say \\"what I know about you\\", \\"your weekly report\\", \\"your check-in\\", \\"the plan\\", \\"how hard it felt\\" (or \\"effort 7/10\\"), \\"during the week\\" and \\"planned\\" instead, and never tell the athlete to open a page by name. Week ids such as 2026-W43 are internal: never write them or a short form such as W43 or W43's. Name a week the way the athlete reads it: \\"this week\\", \\"next week\\", \\"the week of Oct 19\\", \\"Oct 19 – 25\\" or \\"4 weeks out from the race\\". Write plain sentences. No exclamation marks, no sentences that open with \\"Let's\\", and no emoji or decorative symbols. Write dates as \\"Mon Oct 19\\" and ranges as \\"Oct 19 – 25\\", never as 2026-10-19. Lead with the conclusion: the first sentence says what matters most. Give each paragraph one idea and state it in the paragraph's first sentence. Keep paragraphs to at most three sentences, and sentences under 25 words. When you give more than three numbers that belong together, such as splits, paces or heart rates, put them in a Markdown table instead of a sentence. Keep your reasoning as sentences: say why, not just what. The headline is the sentence you would say if you could say only one thing about the week and the plan: one or two plain sentences, no label. Flags are what you are watching, not a summary of the week: at most five, each a few-word title and one line of detail. Choose flags by what the athlete needs to act on. A correction comes first: when the athlete ran a session differently from the plan, flag it, and explain in the analysis what happened and why it matters. Answers are one per question the athlete asked in the weekly check-in or the conversation, with the question quoted exactly as they wrote it. When they asked nothing, leave answers empty. Put the reason for each session in that day's notes: one or two sentences on why this session, this week, under 200 characters. The athlete sees them beside the session. The athlete reads the headline, then the flags, then the answers, then the analysis, and the analysis may be folded away. Make each flag and answer stand on its own: never point to something \\"above\\" or \\"below\\", or to a table in another part. The analysis holds your reasoning. Do not restate the headline, flags or answers in it, and never repeat an answer's table or list there. Do not list the days or describe each session in it, as a list or as a table; the day rows already show them. Safety callouts: - Never diagnose injury, illness, or medical conditions; do not prescribe treatment. - Coach running from the athlete's volunteered context and Tempo-derived training data. - When red flags appear (pain that worsens, chest pain, dizziness, sudden injury, illness), emit safetyCallouts and urge a qualified professional. - Each callout needs a short title, athlete-facing body (escalate, do not diagnose), and optional severity (info|caution|urgent). - Keep each callout body under 600 characters: one or two short paragraphs. - Keep the training plan/reply conservative when callouts are present. Think it through first, then call the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer. Do not write the answer as plain text.", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reviewed week (already happened): 2026-W40\\nPlan week ISO label: 2026-W41\\nPlan week Monday (YYYY-MM-DD): 2026-10-05\\nprescribedWeek.days must use these exact dates Mon–Sun: 2026-10-05, 2026-10-06, 2026-10-07, 2026-10-08, 2026-10-09, 2026-10-10, 2026-10-11\\nPrescribe one coherent week of running training for this athlete.\\nCall the submit_weekly_reply tool exactly once with your full answer.\\nInclude a headline (the one or two sentences you would say if you could say only one thing), flags (what you are watching), answers (one per question the athlete asked, quoted as written) and the analysis (your reasoning).\\nWhen red flags appear, populate safetyCallouts (escalate; do not diagnose).\\nModel packet (redacted; no identity PII or GPS):\\n{\\"dossier\\":{\\"versionId\\":1,\\"body\\":\\"# About you\\\\n\\\\n## Stance\\\\nUse only the athlete's volunteered information and Tempo-derived training context. Coach training decisions; do not diagnose medical conditions or prescribe treatment. If something sounds medical or outside coaching, urge the athlete to seek a qualified professional and keep training conservative.\\\\n\\\\n## Goal\\\\nYou are training for the Lakeshore Marathon on Sun Nov 15, aiming for 3:30. This will be your fourth marathon, and your personal best is 3:41 from two years ago.\\\\n\\\\n## Schedule\\\\nYou run five days a week: Tue, Wed, Thu, Sat and Sun. Mon and Fri are fixed rest days because of work travel.\\\\n\\\\nWeekday runs are early, around 6am before work, capped near an hour. Saturday is an easy hour and Sunday is the long run.\\\\n\\\\nTypical volume is 6 to 7 hours a week, with more on long-run weeks.\\\\n\\\\n## Running history\\\\nYou have run three marathons already, best 3:41. You eased off in June and have built back to about 30 miles a week through July.\\\\n\\\\nRecent weeks show four easy runs of roughly 5 to 6 miles and a long run of about 10.8 miles on Sun Aug 2.\\\\n\\\\n## Injuries and constraints\\\\nYou are healthy right now. You had shin splints in your first marathon block, so you prefer to ramp mileage up carefully.\\\\n\\\\nWork travel takes you away about one week in six. Mon and Fri rest days cannot move.\\\\n\\\\n## Preferences\\\\nYou train mostly on flat paved loops and a canal path, with one hillier route on Thursdays. You want easy days to be properly easy and you are happy to work hard on quality days.\\\\n\\\\nYou like to know why a session is in the plan. Shoes: Brooks Ghost 16 for daily miles, Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 for workouts.\\\\n\\\\n## Current status\\\\nYou are at about 30 miles a week with a long run near 11 miles. Units are miles. Heart rate zones are set custom, with easy running in 112 – 150 bpm.\\"},\\"recapMarkdown\\":\\"# Weekly Recap — 2026-W40 (Mon Sep 28 – Sun Oct 4, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Summary\\\\n\\\\n| Metric | This week | Prev week | 3-wk avg | Δ |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Mileage | 43.98 mi | 34.83 mi | 30.66 mi | +13.3 |\\\\n| Runs | 5 | 5 | 5.0 | +0.0 |\\\\n| Total time | 6h 36m | 5h 20m | 4h 45m | +1h 51m |\\\\n| Total elevation | 787 ft | 722 ft | 664 ft | +124 ft |\\\\n| Relative effort | 317 | 274 | 223 | +94 |\\\\n| Avg easy-run HR | 144 | 144 | 144 | +0 |\\\\n\\\\n## HR zone distribution (week total)\\\\n\\\\nZ1 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  1.1%   (5m)\\\\nZ2 ██████████████████████████░░░░░░░░░  74.8%   (4h 57m)\\\\nZ3 ███████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  18.9%   (1h 15m)\\\\nZ4 ██░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  5.1%   (20m)\\\\nZ5 ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░  0%   (0m)\\\\n\\\\nTarget for marathon base phase: **≥80% in Z1+Z2**. This week: **75.95%**. **Below** typical marathon base target (≥80% Z1+Z2).\\\\n\\\\n## Run-by-run\\\\n\\\\n### Tue Sep 29 — Easy Run — 5.85 mi / 54m 5s\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:15/mi  ·  Avg HR 142 (75.5% max)  ·  Max HR 148  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:14/mi / 9:18/mi / 9:18/mi / 9:17/mi / 9:09/mi / 9:07/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 139.1 → 144 (+4.9 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zones: Z1 1.8% · Z2 98.2% · Z3 0% · Z4 0% · Z5 0%\\\\n\\\\nElevation: ↑108 ft\\\\n\\\\nShoe: Brooks Ghost 16\\\\n\\\\nRelative effort: 38\\\\n\\\\nSimilar route: n/a\\\\n\\\\nRPE: 3/10  ·  Felt: 8/10\\\\n\\\\n### Wed Sep 30 — Easy Run — 7.97 mi / 1h 12m\\\\n\\\\nPace: 9:07/mi  ·  Avg HR 148 (78.7% max)  ·  Max HR 153  ·  Cad 344 spm\\\\n\\\\nSplits: 9:06/mi / 9:06/mi / 9:09/mi / 9:04/mi / 9:09/mi / 9:02/mi / 9:06/mi / 9:02/mi\\\\n\\\\nHR drift: 145 → 149.7 (+4.7 bpm)\\\\n\\\\nTime in zo