COPY public.prescribed_weeks (week_session_id, plan, analysis, settings_proposal_bullets, created_at, updated_at, safety_callouts, read_sections) FROM stdin;
fb524296-d5a5-481d-950c-93d942c0b4f7	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-10", "notes": "Fixed rest with travel. Walk and hydrate if you can.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR 130 – 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-11", "notes": "Easy shakeout to open the week. Keep HR under 150 and start the first mile slow.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-12", "notes": "Standard midweek easy. Last week this run drifted to Z3 — ease into it.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "2 mi WU · 5 x 1 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-08-13", "notes": "Marathon-pace reps with full recovery. Short enough to hit goal pace cleanly in heat.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-08-14", "notes": "Fixed rest for travel. Try to get the strength session in if you are home.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-15", "notes": "Easy hour, legs loose for Sunday. Nothing to prove.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 152", "distanceM": 20921, "structure": "13 mi steady. Start before sunrise, carry fluid."}, "date": "2026-08-16", "notes": "Steady 13 — small bump from last week. No MP finish; just time on feet in heat.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W33"}	## Why this shape\n\nLast week jumped about 10% in miles and the overall effort score climbed well above your 3-week average, so this week holds mileage flat and keeps the quality session shorter. You are healthy and the long run went well, but a second big jump on top of 25°C mornings is where shin trouble tends to creep back in.\n\n## The quality day\n\nThursday moves from a straight tempo to marathon-pace miles with full recoveries. Goal pace for 3:30 is 8:00/mi, and short reps let you touch that pace in the heat without the heart rate drift you would get from a long block. If 8:00 feels forced, run 8:05 – 8:10 and we will build from there.\n\n## Easy running\n\nKeep Tuesday, Wednesday and Saturday properly easy, which for you right now means roughly 9:45 – 10:00/mi and heart rate under 150. Your Wednesday run drifted into Z3 last week; start the first mile slower and let it settle.\n\n## Long run\n\nThirteen miles on Sunday, steady, no marathon-pace finish this week. Start before sunrise if you can, carry fluid, and if the heat index is brutal, cut it to 11 rather than grind.	\N	2026-08-09 20:00:00+00	2026-08-09 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Effort spike last week", "detail": "Overall effort score +33% vs your 3-week average. Holding volume flat this week to absorb it."}, {"title": "Heat on long runs", "detail": "25°C at 6am Sunday is real stress. Start earlier, carry fluid, and judge pace by effort not the watch."}, {"title": "Wednesday drift into Z3", "detail": "17% of Wednesday was Z3. Start the first mile 15 – 20s slower so easy stays easy."}, {"title": "Shin history", "detail": "Mileage ramp stays conservative. No back-to-back jumps while you build toward 40 mi weeks."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Honest read: it is a stretch goal, not a lock. 3:30 needs 8:00/mi for 26.2, and right now your easy pace sits around 9:43 at a heart rate (148) that is already upper-Z2. Your current fitness points closer to a 3:38 – 3:45 range if the race were tomorrow.\\n\\nThe good news is you have about 14 weeks, a solid base, and your long run just went well. If the marathon-pace work on Thursdays lands cleanly over the next 4 – 6 weeks and the long runs grow without breaking you, 3:30 stays on the table. If 8:00/mi still feels like a grind in mid-September, we recalibrate to 3:35 and race smart. I would rather chase 3:30 and hold a backup plan than lock it in now.", "question": "Is 3:30 realistic for me from where I am now?"}], "headline": "Hold mileage flat this week after last week's jump, keep the quality day short and sharp, and let the long run build to 13 easy miles."}
4cf30ecf-244c-4bcd-91f9-23d4ebaaa42b	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-17", "notes": "Fixed rest day. Travel day, nothing to prove.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 135 – 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-18", "notes": "Easy shakeout to open the week. Keep HR under 150 and let pace be whatever that is.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 135 – 148", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-19", "notes": "Standard midweek aerobic run before Thursday's tempo. Nothing creeping into Z3.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "Tempo miles 8:15 – 8:25/mi", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, 4 mi tempo @ 8:15 – 8:25/mi (one continuous block), 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-08-20", "notes": "Continuous tempo to practice pacing discipline after last week's fast 800s. Steady is the win here.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-08-21", "notes": "Fixed rest day. Full recovery before the weekend double.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi easy"}, "date": "2026-08-22", "notes": "Easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy, no pace chasing.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR 142 – 150", "distanceM": 22531, "structure": "14 mi continuous easy"}, "date": "2026-08-23", "notes": "Long run up to 14 mi at the same easy effort as last Sunday. If humid at 6am, start 10 – 20 sec/mi slower.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W34"}	## Why this week looks the way it does\n\nYou handled 37.7 miles with an honest long run and the aerobic signal is clean, so the next step is a small build to about 40 with one controlled tempo. Easy HR has stayed at 144 while easy pace nudged from 9:50 to 9:43, which is exactly the direction you want for a marathon base.\n\nThursday is a tempo rather than reps because you flagged the 800s felt too fast early. Running sustained 4 miles near 8:15 – 8:25 per mile teaches pacing discipline and gives a cleaner read on where fitness sits versus 3:30 goal pace of 8:00/mi. The Endorphin Speed 4 is the right shoe for it.\n\nThe long run grows to 14 miles at the same easy effort you ran Sunday. If the air is thick at 6am again, drop pace by 10 – 20 seconds per mile from the start rather than hanging on and drifting late, which is what the +6.9 bpm drift on Sunday hinted at.\n\nMileage is up roughly 2 miles on last week, well inside a safe ramp given your shin history. Keep the two strength sessions in and the sleep closer to 7.5 if you can; it is the cheapest gain available right now.	\N	2026-08-16 20:00:00+00	2026-08-16 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Tempo pacing discipline", "detail": "Last Thursday's 800s ran 6:36 – 6:41/mi, faster than needed. This week's tempo is deliberately steadier at 8:15 – 8:25/mi."}, {"title": "Long-run HR drift in heat", "detail": "Sunday drifted +6.9 bpm with back half even on pace. If humid, start 10 – 20 sec/mi slower rather than fading late."}, {"title": "Shin history", "detail": "Mileage ramp held to ~2 mi this week. Flag any shin tightness early rather than training through it."}, {"title": "Sleep", "detail": "6.9 hr average is on the low side given the build. Aim for 7.5 where travel allows."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Slow it down from the first mile, do not try to hold pace. In real heat, 10 – 20 seconds per mile slower keeps the aerobic stimulus the same while cutting the drift and the late-run slog you felt Sunday. Target the same easy heart rate (around 148, staying under 150), and let pace fall where it falls. Fitness is built by time at effort, not by splits you fought for in the humidity.", "question": "Should I slow the long run down when it is this hot, or hold the pace?"}], "headline": "You are building cleanly toward Lakeshore: nudge to about 40 miles this week with a controlled tempo on Thursday and a 14-mile long run kept at the same easy effort you ran Sunday."}
f71bdae7-2e7c-4e9b-9573-875357c99847	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-24", "notes": "Fixed rest with travel. Short walk and some hip mobility if you have 10 minutes.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-25", "notes": "Easy opener. First mile deliberately slow — let HR settle before you find a rhythm.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-26", "notes": "Standard midweek easy. Keep it in Z2 — this one has crept into Z3 twice recently.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "2 mi WU · 3 x 1 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-08-27", "notes": "Short goal-pace reps with full recovery. Enough to feel 8:00/mi without draining the week.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-08-28", "notes": "Fixed rest. Good day for the second short strength session if you are home.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-08-29", "notes": "Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat, cut to 4 miles — no hero stuff.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 152", "distanceM": 19312, "structure": "12 mi steady, no MP finish"}, "date": "2026-08-30", "notes": "Steady 12. Start before sunrise, carry fluid, hold HR under 150 through mile 8 then let it climb naturally.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W35"}	## Why a cutback now\n\nYou jumped 7 miles week over week on 6.4 hours of sleep and high work stress, and Saturday's easy run already felt like work. That is the moment to pull back a notch, not push through. The pattern we want in a build is three up, one down, and you have had three up weeks in a row.\n\n## What stays, what shrinks\n\nLong run holds near 12 miles so you keep the aerobic stimulus but with less total load around it. Thursday's workout drops to three marathon-pace miles with full recoveries — enough to touch 8:00/mi cleanly without the Z4 time you got from the longer tempo. Easy days stay the same length but I want them genuinely easy, HR under 150, pace closer to 9:50 than 9:30.\n\n## Looking ahead\n\nIf this week lands well, next week goes back up toward 40 with a 16-mile long run and a longer quality session. If sleep stays short or anything in the shins whispers, we hold flat another week — the race is still 11 weeks out and the base is there.	\N	2026-08-23 20:00:00+00	2026-08-23 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Cutback week", "detail": "Mileage drops from 42.5 to around 35. You asked for a lighter week and the numbers back it up."}, {"title": "Sleep and stress", "detail": "6.4 hr average with high work stress. If sleep stays under 6 hr, swap Thursday's reps for easy miles."}, {"title": "Long-run drift", "detail": "Sunday drifted +7.3 bpm over 15 miles. Start slower this Sunday and keep HR under 150 through mile 8."}, {"title": "Strength", "detail": "One of two done last week. Aim for both short sessions; skip rather than squeeze a late evening one."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Protect the long run and one quality session; drop easy miles first.\\n\\nMy priority order when a travel week compresses:\\n\\n| Priority | Keep | Why |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | Sunday long run | Biggest marathon-specific stimulus. Move it to Saturday if Sunday is a travel day. |\\n| 2 | One quality session | Keeps goal pace in the legs. Shorten rather than skip — three MP miles is still useful. |\\n| 3 | Easy aerobic time | Nice to have. A 30-minute hotel treadmill run counts. |\\n\\nDo not try to make up missed miles the following week — stacking a travel week with a catch-up week is how the shin trouble came back last time. If you lose two or more runs, treat the next week as a soft restart: easy days easy, long run no more than last completed long plus a mile.", "question": "My schedule gets tight when I travel for work. How should I handle missed runs?"}], "headline": "Last week was a big jump on tired legs and short sleep, so this week pulls mileage back about 15% and keeps the quality light while the long run holds."}
ba71dfa0-e641-49e5-ab16-05476f66eca8	{"days": [{"date": "2026-08-31", "notes": "Fixed rest with travel. Walk, hydrate, early night before Tuesday.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35 – 9:50/mi, HR 135 – 148", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-01", "notes": "Easy opener. HR under 148, pace is whatever that gives you.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35 – 9:50/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-02", "notes": "Midweek aerobic run before Thursday's quality. Nothing creeping into Z3.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries ~10:00/mi", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, 2 x 3 mi @ 8:00/mi w/ 3 min easy jog, 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-09-03", "notes": "Marathon-pace reps. First real touch of 8:00/mi this block — settle in, do not force it.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Fixed rest. Travel day. Eat and sleep well for Sunday.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi easy"}, "date": "2026-09-05", "notes": "Easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy, no chasing.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "First 13 mi 9:40 – 9:55/mi, last 3 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi", "distanceM": 25750, "structure": "13 mi steady easy, then 3 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi (no CD needed)"}, "date": "2026-09-06", "notes": "16 mi with last 3 at MP. First MP-on-tired-legs rep. Start honest, finish strong — fuel plan in the answer.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W36"}	## Why this shape\n\nLast week was a planned step back and you came out of it feeling bouncy, so this week rebuilds to around 42 miles with the long run going to 16 and one quality session touching marathon pace. You are 11 weeks out from Lakeshore, which is the right window to start layering goal-pace work into the long run instead of only doing it on Thursday.\n\n## The quality day\n\nThursday is marathon-pace reps: 2 x 3 miles at 8:00/mi with 3 minutes easy jog between. Two three-mile blocks are long enough to feel like race rhythm but short enough that you are not hanging on in the second half, which is where pacing errors show up. If 8:00 feels forced in the first rep, settle at 8:05 and we will tighten it over the next few weeks.\n\n## The long run\n\nSixteen miles Sunday, mostly steady, with the last 3 miles lifted to marathon-pace effort. This is the first time this block we are putting goal pace on tired legs, and it is the single most useful workout between here and taper. Start the first 10 miles honest and easy (9:40 – 9:55), then roll into 8:00 – 8:05 for the finish.\n\n## Easy running and context\n\nEasy pace has crept from 9:50 to 9:34 at the same 144 HR, which is exactly the aerobic drift you want. Keep Tue, Wed and Sat properly easy — if HR climbs toward 150 early, slow down. Fueling for the long runs matters more now that we are over two hours, and I have put specifics in the answer.	\N	2026-08-30 20:00:00+00	2026-08-30 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Long-run drift", "detail": "Sunday's HR drifted +8.5 bpm over 10.86 mi. Start the 16-miler conservatively so the MP finish is clean, not a grind."}, {"title": "First MP-on-tired-legs", "detail": "Last 3 mi of Sunday at 8:00 – 8:05/mi is a new stress. If it falls apart, that is useful data, not a problem."}, {"title": "Fueling plan", "detail": "Over two hours of running now needs carbs and fluid in. Details in the answer."}, {"title": "Shin history", "detail": "Jump from 33.7 to ~42 mi is in bounds but at the top of safe. Any shin tightness, flag it immediately."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Short version: once a long run goes over about 75 minutes, you want roughly 30 – 60 g of carbohydrate per hour and fluid to thirst, plus a small amount of sodium if it is warm.\\n\\nFor Sunday's 16-miler, a workable plan looks like this:\\n\\n| When | What |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 30 – 60 min before | Small carb snack: banana, toast with honey, or half a bagel. Water. |\\n| Every 25 – 30 min on the run | One gel (~22 – 25 g carbs) with a few sips of water |\\n| Fluid | 400 – 600 ml/hr sipped, more if humid. Carry a handheld or soft flask. |\\n| Sodium | If sweating heavily, an electrolyte tab in the bottle (~300 – 500 mg sodium/hr) |\\n\\nPractical notes. Take the first gel at 25 – 30 minutes, not when you feel flat — by then it is too late. Practise with the exact gel brand and flavour you plan to use on race day, because marathon day is not the time to find out your stomach hates a flavour. Maurten, SIS and Gu are all fine; pick one and stick with it for the block.\\n\\nFor Thursday's workout you do not need fuel on the run, but eat something small 30 – 60 min before so you can actually hit pace.\\n\\nIf anything new causes stomach trouble, back off that product and try another — this is a coaching question, not a medical one, but if gut issues persist talk to a sports dietitian.", "question": "What should I be eating and drinking on the long runs? I've mostly gone without so far."}], "headline": "You came out of the cutback week fresh, so this week rebuilds to about 42 miles with a 16-mile long run that finishes at marathon pace — the first real goal-pace work on tired legs."}
48424895-cef1-450a-ab68-121423204d3a	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-07", "notes": "Fixed rest with travel. Short walk and hip mobility if you have ten minutes.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-08", "notes": "Easy opener after a big week. First mile at 10:00/mi — do not chase pace, let HR settle.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-09", "notes": "Standard midweek aerobic. Keep this one in Z2 — it slid into Z3 last Wednesday.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 9:45 – 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "2 mi WU · 4 x 1 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-09-10", "notes": "MP reps with short recoveries. Touch 8:00/mi again without stacking a second long MP block on Sunday.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-11", "notes": "Fixed rest. Good day for the second strength session if you are home.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-12", "notes": "Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat, cut to 4 — no hero stuff.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 152", "distanceM": 22531, "structure": "14 mi steady. One gel at ~45 min, one at ~90 min, with fluid."}, "date": "2026-09-13", "notes": "Steady 14, no MP finish. Fuel at 45 and 90 minutes to start building the race-day habit.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W37"}	## Why a cutback now\n\nYou went from 33.7 miles to 44.4 in a single step, with a 16-mile long run that finished at marathon pace. That is a lot of new load in one week, and the +13.4 bpm drift on Sunday tells me the back half cost more than the pace suggests. A small pullback now protects the gains and keeps the shins quiet.\n\n## What I like\n\nMarathon pace on Thursday sat at 7:58 – 8:01/mi with an average HR of 153 and you called it a 6/10. That is the first clean read we have had on goal pace, and the fact that you felt controlled is the sentence I care about most. Easy pace has drifted from 9:43 to 9:30 at the same 144 HR, which is honest aerobic progress.\n\n## What this week does\n\nMileage lands near 38 with the long run at 14 and no marathon-pace finish. Thursday is four MP miles broken into reps so you touch 8:00/mi again without stacking two hard long efforts back to back. If next Sunday feels crisp, the week after we go back up — likely 16 again, this time with a longer MP block built in.\n\n## Easy running\n\nThe two easy days drifted into Z3 last week (Wed 19% Z3, long run 19% Z3). That is where cutback weeks earn their keep. Start the first mile at 10:00/mi or slower on every easy day and let HR, not pace, set the ceiling.	\N	2026-09-06 20:00:00+00	2026-09-06 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Big mileage jump", "detail": "44.4 mi was +31% on the prior week. Pulling back to ~38 this week to consolidate before pushing again."}, {"title": "Long-run HR drift", "detail": "Sunday drifted +13.4 bpm, above the ≤10 target. Partly the MP finish, partly heat — watching this on the next long run."}, {"title": "Easy days creeping", "detail": "Wed easy run and the long run both sat ~19% in Z3. Easy means under 150 bpm, pace is whatever that is."}, {"title": "Z1+Z2 at 74.6%", "detail": "Below the 80% base target. Natural consequence of the MP work, but worth keeping an eye on."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Yes, start practising now. Take one gel around 45 minutes and a second around 90 minutes on Sunday's 14. The point is not fuel need on a 14-miler — it is teaching your stomach to take sugar while running, so it is a non-event on race day when you will need four or five of them. Use whatever brand you plan to carry at Lakeshore and wash it down with a few sips of water or sports drink.", "question": "Is it worth trying a gel on the long run next week, or keep it simple?"}], "headline": "Big jump last week landed well, so this week is a planned step down to let the gains stick before we push the long run past 16 again. Marathon pace at 8:00/mi is now a real tool, not a hope."}
b992919d-3a13-4d00-bfe2-bff4ac590b02	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-14", "notes": "Fixed rest and travel. Book a physio appointment today if you can.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:10/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 4828, "structure": "3 mi easy on flat pavement"}, "date": "2026-09-15", "notes": "Short test run. If Achilles is above 2/10 or sharpens, stop and walk back — no heroics.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:10/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi flat easy"}, "date": "2026-09-16", "notes": "Only if Tuesday ran clean. Flat loop, steady, no pickups.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Skip calf/Achilles loading this week. Hips and core only, light."}, {"date": "2026-09-17", "notes": "Extra rest instead of the usual Thursday quality. The tendon needs the recovery window more than the session.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"date": "2026-09-18", "notes": "Fixed rest, travel day. Walk a bit; keep moving gently.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:10/mi", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi flat easy"}, "date": "2026-09-19", "notes": "Easy flat miles. Skip if the Achilles was worse during the week.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:10/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "8 mi steady easy on flat pavement"}, "date": "2026-09-20", "notes": "Short long run, all easy, no MP finish. Cut to 6 if any Achilles signal in the first mile.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W38"}	## Why this shape\n\nYou stopped Thursday's session because the Achilles got sharper with pace, and Saturday it was stiff for a mile before settling. That is the classic early-warning pattern and the right response is a short, soft week — not a lost block. The race is nine weeks out and your aerobic base is in good shape, so losing a sharpening week costs you nothing.\n\nQuality is out this week. Pace changes and push-off load the Achilles the most, and that is exactly what provoked it. We hold easy aerobic running at flat routes only, and skip the Thursday hill loop.\n\n## How to run this week\n\nUse a simple traffic-light rule each day. Pain under 2/10 that eases in the first mile and does not get worse while running: go. Pain 3/10 or climbing during the run: stop and walk home. Any sharp pain, swelling, or morning limp: do not run and get it looked at.\n\nShoes stay in the Ghost 16 all week — more stack, less heel drop stress than the Speed 4. Skip calf raises and anything that loads the Achilles hard until a professional clears you. Short, gentle walking is fine and helpful.\n\n## What's next\n\nIf the Achilles settles cleanly by Sunday, next week we rebuild to a normal easy week and then layer quality back in the week after. If it is still grumbling, we hold easy or cross-train longer. Nine weeks is enough runway to protect this and still run 3:30 — pushing through it now is what turns a tight tendon into a six-week problem.	\N	2026-09-13 20:00:00+00	2026-09-13 20:00:00+00	[{"body": "Pain that sharpens with pace and lingers as morning stiffness is worth a professional look, not a wait-and-see. Please book a sports physio or sports medicine doctor in the next few days — they can tell you what's going on and what loading is safe.\\n\\nStop running and seek same-day care if you get sudden sharp pain, a pop or snap sensation, visible swelling, or you can't push off the foot normally. I'm coaching the training around this; the medical read needs to come from a qualified professional.", "title": "Get the Achilles assessed this week", "severity": "caution"}]	{"flags": [{"title": "Achilles — pain-dependent week", "detail": "Right Achilles tightened with pace Thu and was stiff Sat. All quality pulled. Run only if pain stays under 2/10 and does not worsen."}, {"title": "See a sports physio", "detail": "Get it assessed early this week. Nine weeks to race gives time to fix it; another hard session on it does not."}, {"title": "Shoes and routes", "detail": "Ghost 16 only. Flat paved loops. Skip the Thursday hill route — climbs load the Achilles."}, {"title": "No calf loading", "detail": "Hold off heavy calf raises, jumps, strides, and sprints until you are cleared and pain-free running."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Easy running is okay this week **only** if the Achilles behaves. Use this rule each run:\\n\\n| Pain during run | What to do |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Under 2/10, eases after first mile | Continue as planned |\\n| 3/10 or climbing | Stop and walk home |\\n| Sharp, or morning limp next day | Do not run, rest and get it assessed |\\n\\nI would also take Thursday as a second rest day this week to give it an extra 48-hour window. No pace changes, no hills, no strides. Short walks and gentle ankle mobility are fine. If two consecutive runs feel clean, we are on track to rebuild next week.", "question": "Should I stop running for a few days, or keep going with easy runs?"}, {"answer": "That is a question for a sports physio, not me — and I would book one this week rather than wait. I can't diagnose anything, but the pattern coaches watch for is: pain that sharpens with pace, morning stiffness that takes more than a few steps to clear, visible swelling or a lump on the tendon, pain when you press it, or pain going up stairs or doing a single-leg calf raise. Any of those earn a visit.\\n\\nThe reassuring signs are: tightness that eases within the first mile of easy running, no morning limp, no swelling, and settles with a day or two off. You are in a mixed picture right now, which is why we train conservatively and get professional eyes on it.", "question": "How do I know if this is serious?"}], "headline": "The Achilles signal from Thursday is the whole story this week — we pull quality out, keep running only if pain stays low and not worsening, and you get it looked at by a sports physio."}
2453cc65-d713-4a3d-b143-23203b4e72e5	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-21", "notes": "Fixed rest with travel. Heel drops morning and evening.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-22", "notes": "Easy opener. First mile at 10:00/mi — check stiffness as you go, let HR settle before finding rhythm.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:45 – 10:00/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-23", "notes": "Standard midweek aerobic. Keep it honest in Z2 — this run crept to 15% Z3 last week.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "Tempo 8:15 – 8:25/mi, WU/CD ~9:45/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "2 mi WU, 2 mi tempo @ 8:15 – 8:25/mi, 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-09-24", "notes": "First tempo back. Small on purpose — 8:15 – 8:25/mi, not MP. Stop if the Achilles sharpens with pace.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "notes": "Fixed rest. Second strength session if you are home.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:50 – 10:00/mi", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-26", "notes": "Easy hour before Sunday. If legs feel flat after Thursday, drop to 4 miles — no hero stuff.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 152", "distanceM": 19312, "structure": "12 mi continuous easy"}, "date": "2026-09-27", "notes": "Steady 12, no MP finish. Fuel at ~45 and ~90 minutes with water, keep HR under 152 through mile 8.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W39"}	## Where we are\n\nYou lost about a week of load but the aerobic base is intact. Easy pace is still 9:20 – 9:30/mi at HR 142 – 143, and the Sunday long run drifted a reasonable +8 bpm over 8 miles. That is a healthy platform to build from with eight weeks until Lakeshore.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nThe physio cleared one short tempo, so Thursday is deliberately small: a 2-mile tempo at 8:15 – 8:25/mi, not marathon pace. Short enough to test the Achilles under load, long enough to feel like a workout. If morning stiffness returns at any point before Thursday, swap it for an easy 5.\n\nThe long run moves back to 12 miles at pure easy effort. No marathon pace finish this week — we need two clean weeks of tempo and long run before layering goal pace on tired legs again. If Sunday goes well, the week of Sep 28 goes to 14 and we start reintroducing MP work the week after.\n\n## Easy running\n\nThree of the four easy runs last week crept toward 9:17 – 9:20/mi. That is sharper than it needs to be in a rebuild week. Open every run at 10:00/mi for the first mile and let HR, not pace, decide the rest.\n\n## Keep doing\n\nTwice-daily heel drops, the two short strength sessions, and the pre-run stiffness check. If first-mile stiffness ever rises above a 3/10 or sharpens with pace, stop the run and tell me — that is the signal the physio gave you, and it is the right one.	\N	2026-09-20 20:00:00+00	2026-09-20 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Achilles check before every run", "detail": "Morning stiffness should stay gone. Any sharpening with pace means stop and reassess, especially Thursday."}, {"title": "Easy pace drifting fast", "detail": "Easy runs sat at 9:17 – 9:23/mi last week. Open at 10:00/mi and let HR cap the rest, not pace."}, {"title": "Tempo is a test, not a workout", "detail": "Thursday's 2 miles is to see how the Achilles handles sustained effort. Hold 8:15 – 8:25/mi — do not drift toward MP."}, {"title": "Long run back to easy only", "detail": "12 mi steady, no MP finish. We need two clean weeks before putting goal pace on tired legs again."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Yes, Thursday. I have kept it small on purpose: 2 mi warm-up, 2 mi tempo at 8:15 – 8:25/mi, 1 mi cool-down, in the Endorphin Speed 4.\\n\\nTwo things to respect. One, this is a tempo, not marathon pace — do not let it drift toward 8:00/mi because the shoe feels fast. Two, if first-mile stiffness is above a 2/10 or the Achilles sharpens as you lift the pace, stop and jog home easy. That is exactly what the physio told you and it is the right rule.", "question": "The physio is happy for me to try a short tempo next week. Can we put one in?"}, {"answer": "Not yet. 3:30 is still on the table and I am not moving off it this week.\\n\\nHere is the honest read. You lost about one week of load, not fitness — easy pace and HR are unchanged, and the 16-mile long run with MP finish on Sep 6 showed the engine is there. Eight weeks is enough to rebuild if the Achilles stays quiet.\\n\\nThe decision point is the week of Oct 12, roughly four weeks out. By then we need to have put together one 18 – 20 mile long run with a solid MP block and a longer MP session midweek. If the Achilles cooperates and those land, 3:30 stays the plan. If we lose more time, we talk about either a slightly softer goal (3:35) or a run-by-feel race with 3:30 as a stretch. I will flag it clearly when we get there — you will not be guessing.", "question": "Does the missed week change what is realistic for race day?"}], "headline": "Achilles has settled and the physio has cleared a short tempo, so this week we rebuild carefully: steady easy miles, a 2-mile tempo on Thursday, and a 12-mile long run."}
8d1621a0-dfc2-47d7-ac36-0c58953ae4b1	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-28", "notes": "Fixed rest and travel. Heel drops morning and evening.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30 – 9:50/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy on flat pavement"}, "date": "2026-09-29", "notes": "Easy opener. First mile deliberately slow to let the tendon warm through.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:25 – 9:45/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-09-30", "notes": "Midweek aerobic. Keep this in Z2 — it drifted to Z3 last week.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Short strength: hips, core, slow heel drops."}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries 9:45/mi", "distanceM": 12070, "structure": "2 mi WU · 2 x 2 mi at 8:00/mi w/ 3 min easy jog · 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-10-01", "notes": "Longer MP touch than last week, still short of September's tempo. Flat canal only — no hills.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "notes": "Fixed rest, travel. Walk, eat, sleep well for Sunday.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30 – 9:50/mi", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi easy"}, "date": "2026-10-03", "notes": "Short easy hour on legs to set up Sunday. Properly easy.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Second strength session: heel drops, hips, core."}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "First 12 mi 9:30 – 9:50/mi, last 2 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi", "distanceM": 22531, "structure": "12 mi steady easy, then 2 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi"}, "date": "2026-10-04", "notes": "14 mi with a short MP finish — first MP-on-tired-legs since the Achilles. Drop the MP if anything whispers in the first 10.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W40"}	## Why this shape\n\nLast week was the first full load since the Achilles flare and it held together: tempo at 7:35/mi, long run at 9:18/mi with only +8.8 bpm drift, and no body-check flags. That earns a careful step up, not a leap. Seven weeks to race day means two more build weeks, then a peak block, then taper.\n\n## The quality day\n\nThursday is 2 x 2 miles at marathon pace with a 3-minute jog between. That is a longer continuous touch of 8:00/mi than the three Thursday tempo miles you ran last week, but still well short of the 8-mile MP session from early September. Stay on the flat canal loop, not the Thursday hills — pace changes and climbs are what loaded the tendon last time.\n\n## The long run\n\nFourteen miles Sunday with the last 2 at marathon pace. This is the first MP-on-tired-legs rep since the Achilles episode, kept deliberately short so you finish wanting more. If anything whispers in the tendon during the first 10 miles, drop the MP finish and jog it home — no points for toughing it out this far from the race.\n\n## Guardrails\n\nKeep the heel drops going daily, keep the Ghost 16 for everything except Thursday, and keep easy days honest (HR under 150). Wednesday drifted to 13.6% Z3 last week — fine, but let's keep it Z2 this week with Thursday getting harder.	\N	2026-09-27 20:00:00+00	2026-09-27 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Achilles maintenance", "detail": "Clean week but the tendon is still the limiter. Keep heel drops daily and flag any morning stiffness before it builds."}, {"title": "Easy-day creep", "detail": "Wednesday slid into Z3 (13.6%) again. Hold it under 150 bpm so Thursday's quality has somewhere to go."}, {"title": "MP returns to the long run", "detail": "First MP finish since early Sept. Short on purpose — 2 miles, not 3, to re-introduce the stimulus."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Yes — treat this as ongoing maintenance, not a problem you've closed out.\\n\\n- **Heel drops daily**, both straight-leg and bent-knee, through race day. Two sets of 15 each side is plenty. Your physio set them for a reason; they are the single highest-value thing you can do.\\n- **Morning stiffness is your dashboard.** If the first few steps out of bed feel normal, you are winning. If you notice a limp or sharp pain in the first 20 steps, that is the day to tell me and ease back.\\n- **Shoes:** stay in the Ghost 16 for all easy and long miles. The Speed 4 only comes out for the Thursday workout. Don't add a new shoe between now and the race.\\n- **Hills and surges** are what provoked it. Keep Thursday on the flat canal and skip the hillier Thursday route until after Lakeshore.\\n- **Warm up a little longer** before quality — an extra 5 minutes of easy jogging before you pick up the pace is cheap insurance.\\n- **Race day:** no last-minute shoe swap, no aggressive first mile. The tendon will tolerate your current load; it won't tolerate a surprise.\\n\\nIf anything changes — swelling, a limp, sharper pain under load — check back with your physio before we adjust training.", "question": "Is there anything I should keep doing for the Achilles through race day?"}], "headline": "Clean week back on track — the Achilles stayed quiet through a real tempo, so we build cautiously to around 39 miles with marathon-pace work returning to the long run."}
199f5fbb-f5ad-4732-93ab-e9961070e6c3	{"days": [{"date": "2026-10-05", "notes": "Fixed rest, travel day. Walk if you can, light hip and ankle mobility in the evening.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:35 – 9:50/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-10-06", "notes": "Easy opener after Sunday's long effort. First mile at 9:50/mi, let HR settle before finding rhythm.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30 – 9:45/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-10-07", "notes": "Standard aerobic hour before Thursday. Keep this one honest in Z2 — it crept to Z3 last week.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "quality", "shoes": "Saucony Endorphin Speed 4", "paceOrHr": "MP 8:00/mi, recoveries ~9:45/mi", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "2 mi WU @ 9:45/mi, 4 x 1 mi @ 8:00/mi w/ 2 min easy jog, 1 mi CD"}, "date": "2026-10-08", "notes": "Marathon-pace reps instead of a continuous block. Shorter pieces this week protects Sunday — settle at 8:05 if 8:00 feels forced.", "dayType": "quality"}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "notes": "Fixed rest, travel day. Good day for one strength session if you are home — hips, core, calves.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-10-10", "notes": "Easy hour on legs before Sunday. Properly easy — if legs feel flat, drop to 4 miles.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "First 11 mi 9:40 – 9:55/mi, last 3 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi, HR under 152 through mile 8", "distanceM": 22531, "structure": "11 mi steady easy, then 3 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi"}, "date": "2026-10-11", "notes": "14 with last 3 at MP. Open at 9:45/mi, do not touch 9:30 before mile 4, then roll into 8:00 – 8:05 for the finish. Fuel at 45 and 90 min.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W41"}	## Why we are stepping back\n\nYou jumped from 30 to 44 miles in one week with a 17-mile long run that finished at goal pace. That is a lot of new load, and the long-run drift of +15.9 bpm is the one line in your week I want to respect. The legs felt good on Monday, which is exactly why now is the time to consolidate, not keep piling on.\n\n## Why 14 miles on Sunday\n\nThe next two weeks are where the block earns the race, and both will carry 18 – 20 mile long runs with longer marathon-pace blocks. Running 15 or 16 this week would spend fitness I need you to have on Oct 18 and Oct 25. Fourteen with a 3-mile marathon-pace finish keeps you in rhythm without stacking two heavy Sundays back to back.\n\n## About that drift\n\nFifteen bpm over 17 miles usually means one of three things: opening pace was a touch quick, fueling was late, or the MP finish ran hot. Your front half was 9:10/mi which is honest, so my best guess is the MP miles pulled the average up. On Sunday, set a hard rule: no faster than 9:45/mi for the first two miles, and the MP block starts at 8:05, not 8:00.\n\n## Thursday as reps, not a block\n\nFour one-mile reps at 8:00/mi gives you the same seven miles of session volume as last Thursday but with recovery jogs built in. That means less cumulative fatigue going into Sunday while still rehearsing the pace. If the first rep feels forced, settle at 8:05 — we have four weeks of harder MP work ahead and nothing is proven today.\n\n## Easy days\n\nEasy pace drifted to 9:14/mi last week at HR 144. The HR is fine, the pace is sharper than you need. Start the first mile at 9:50 or slower on every easy day, let HR settle, and let that decide the rest. The point of easy days this week is to arrive at Thursday and Sunday with legs that want to work.	\N	2026-10-04 20:00:00+00	2026-10-04 20:00:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Long-run drift above target", "detail": "Sunday climbed +15.9 bpm over 17 miles. Open the next long run at 9:45/mi and start the MP block at 8:05, not 8:00."}, {"title": "Easy pace creeping sharp", "detail": "Easy days drifted to 9:14/mi at HR 144. First mile of every easy run opens at 9:50/mi, let HR set the ceiling."}, {"title": "Big mileage jump", "detail": "30 to 44 miles in one week is a lot. Cutting to 39 this week to protect the next two peak weeks."}, {"title": "Six weeks to Lakeshore", "detail": "Peak load lands Oct 12 – 25, then a three-week taper. Sleep and strength matter more every week from here."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Here is the shape I am aiming for between now and Nov 15. Numbers will adjust to how each week lands, but the arc is set.\\n\\n| Week | Miles | Long run | Quality focus |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Oct 5 – 11 | ~39 | 14 w/ last 3 at MP | 4 x 1 mi at MP |\\n| Oct 12 – 18 | ~46 | 18 w/ last 5 at MP | 2 x 3 mi at MP |\\n| Oct 19 – 25 | ~44 | 20 w/ 6 miles of MP mixed in | 4 mi continuous at MP |\\n| Oct 26 – Nov 1 | ~36 | 15 steady, no MP finish | 3 mi tempo |\\n| Nov 2 – 8 | ~28 | 10 w/ 3 at MP | Short MP strides |\\n| Nov 9 – 15 | ~16 + race | 4 mi shakeout Sat | Race Sun Nov 15 |\\n\\nThe two weeks of Oct 12 – 25 are the ones that matter most. Oct 18 is your biggest confidence long run — 18 miles with a five-mile marathon-pace block will tell us if 3:30 is the right target or if we dial to 3:32 – 3:34. Oct 25 is the longest session of the whole block at 20 miles, and after that we taper and you only need to not undo the work.\\n\\nTwo rules for the taper: miles come down, intensity stays. You will feel sluggish in the week of Nov 2 and that is normal — it is the taper, not lost fitness. Protect sleep, keep fueling the long runs, and we will talk race-day pacing and warm-up specifics around Nov 2.", "question": "How should the final weeks before the race look?"}], "headline": "After the biggest week in months, we consolidate at 39 miles with a 14-mile long run and shorter marathon-pace reps, so you arrive at the two peak weeks of Oct 12 – 25 fresh instead of fried."}
0e0e6db2-007d-47cf-8fec-43337d6ca624	{"days": [{"date": "2026-10-12", "notes": "Fixed rest and travel. Walk, hydrate, bed early — the 20 miler is still in your legs.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30 – 9:50/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi steady easy on flat pavement"}, "date": "2026-10-13", "notes": "Easy opener. First mile at 9:40/mi, no pace chasing — HR sets the ceiling.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:30 – 9:50/mi, HR under 150", "distanceM": 11265, "structure": "7 mi steady easy"}, "date": "2026-10-14", "notes": "Midweek aerobic. Keep this in Z2 — it kept leaking to Z3 last week.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Short strength: hips, core, heel drops."}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi, HR under 148", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi steady easy on flat pavement"}, "date": "2026-10-15", "notes": "Short easy to fit the 40-minute window. The MP touch moves to Sunday — this one is just rhythm.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"date": "2026-10-16", "notes": "Fixed rest, travel. Eat and sleep well — Sunday is the key session.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "9:40 – 9:55/mi", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi easy"}, "date": "2026-10-17", "notes": "Easy miles to open the legs. If they still feel flat, cut to 3 — no hero stuff.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "Second strength session: heel drops, hips, core."}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "First 12 mi 9:30 – 9:50/mi, HR under 150. Last 4 mi 8:00 – 8:05/mi.", "distanceM": 25750, "structure": "12 mi steady easy, then 4 mi at MP 8:00 – 8:05/mi. Gel at 45 and 90 min, drink to a schedule."}, "date": "2026-10-18", "notes": "Open at 9:45/mi, nothing faster than 9:30 before mile 4, then roll into MP. If the Achilles whispers in the first 10 miles, drop the MP and jog home.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W42"}	## Where we are\n\nFour weeks and five days out from race day, with your biggest long run of the block in the bank. The 20 miler is a confidence builder: last 4 miles at 8:00/mi, finishing on top of goal pace. The Achilles stayed quiet all the way. That is the sentence I come back to.\n\nThe things to tidy are drift and intensity distribution, not fitness. Long run drift of +16.9 bpm is wider than I want and matches what we saw the week before. That is partly the 20 mile distance and partly a Thursday that cost more than it should have.\n\n## Why a cutback\n\nYou stepped from 44 to 45 miles with a 20 mile long run that finished at goal pace, then slept 6.8 hours and skipped a strength day. Heavy legs on Monday are the normal receipt. Pushing straight into another big week to chase mileage is how this block ends badly.\n\nSo volume comes down to about 39 miles and the quality shrinks on purpose. Peak week lands Oct 19 – 25 with the final long effort, then we taper. We earn more by absorbing this long run than by adding to it.\n\n## Thursday is the correction\n\nThe Thursday session read as five reps around 6:55 – 7:02/mi off 9:51 jogs, with max HR 184 and a 9/10 effort. That is 10K pace or faster, not marathon pace. For a 3:30 goal we do not need to visit that gear again in this block, and the cost of visiting it is the drift we are seeing on Sunday.\n\nThis week Thursday becomes 4 continuous miles at 8:00/mi — the exact pace you will hold for 3:30. Lock in the feel of it, not the hero workout. If pace drifts under 7:55, back off; the point is a locked rhythm, not a time trial.\n\n## The long run\n\nSixteen miles with the last 3 at marathon pace. Shorter than last week by design, but the MP block comes earlier in the run so you touch goal pace on tired legs again without compounding fatigue. Fuel at 45 and 90 minutes, drink to a schedule, and treat the first 10 miles as truly easy under HR 150.\n\n## Easy running\n\nZ1 + Z2 was 68.7% last week against the 80% target. The Wednesday run drifted to the top of Z2 and the long run spent 11% in Z4. Start every easy day at 9:40/mi for the first mile and let heart rate — not pace — decide the rest.\n\n## Housekeeping\n\nProtect sleep this week; you cannot cash into peak week on 6.8 hours. Second strength session goes on Saturday after the easy run, not Friday — travel keeps breaking it. Shoes as usual: Ghost 16 for everything except Thursday.	\N	2026-10-11 20:00:00+00	2026-10-14 22:40:00+00	\N	{"flags": [{"title": "Thursday ran too hot", "detail": "Reps at 6:55 – 7:02/mi with max HR 184 at 9/10 is 10K pace, not marathon pace. We don't need that gear again. Thursday goes back to 8:00/mi."}, {"title": "Long run drift above target", "detail": "+16.9 bpm over 20 mi, above the ≤10 target and the second week in a row. Partly distance, partly accumulated load. The shorter MP finish this week is the fix."}, {"title": "Easy time only 68.7% Z1+Z2", "detail": "Target is 80%. Wednesday and the long run keep leaking into Z3/Z4. First mile at 9:40/mi, then let HR cap the rest."}, {"title": "Sleep at 6.8 hrs", "detail": "Fine for a cutback week but not for peak week. Aim for 7+ most nights before Oct 19 – 25."}, {"title": "Skipped Friday strength", "detail": "Travel keeps killing it. Moving the second session to Saturday after the easy run to make it stick."}], "answers": [{"answer": "Yes. The strongest evidence is Sunday: you ran the last 4 miles of a 20 miler at 8:00 – 8:04/mi and called it a 7/10. That is goal pace on the most tired legs of the block, and you held it cleanly.\\n\\nThe supporting signs point the same way. Easy pace has moved from 9:24/mi to 9:12/mi at the same 144 HR since early September. Thursday's MP work in early October sat at 7:55 – 8:00/mi with HR 151 — well under threshold. For a 3:41 PB from two years ago, you are now a different runner.\\n\\nWhat we need to work on is the back half of the long run. The +16.9 bpm drift is the one caveat, and the plan from here is designed around fixing it: shorter MP blocks earlier in the long run, honest easy days, and a real taper. If the next long run (Oct 19 – 25) finishes clean, 3:30 is the plan on race day.", "question": "Is my 3:30 target still sensible after this block?"}, {"answer": "Yes. Completely normal, especially for the longest training run you have done in two years. The 20 miler is the single hardest aerobic effort in a marathon block, and 24 – 48 hours of heavy legs is the expected tax.\\n\\nWhat I would watch is the trajectory, not the Monday. Heavy on Monday, better on Tuesday, close to normal by Wednesday means you absorbed the work. If Tuesday is worse than Monday, or if anything sharpens from heaviness into a specific pain point, tell me and we adjust.\\n\\nOne nudge: with sleep at 6.8 hours, legs recover slower. The best thing you can do this week is bank an extra 30 – 60 minutes in bed. It will do more than any session I could write.", "question": "Is heavy legs on Monday normal after a 20 miler?"}], "headline": "The 20 miler was the headline of the block and your 3:30 is on track; this week we cut back, lock Thursday into true goal pace, and absorb the work before peaking Oct 19 – 25."}
\.


